window.REVIEWERS = {"datastruc": {"file": "datastrucreviewer.pdf", "html": "<h1>DATASTRC Module 5: Binary Trees – Complete Reviewer</h1>\n<p><em>Data Structures and Algorithms · Weeks 13–15 · plus Additional Notes (Stack or Queue</em> <em>exercise)</em></p>\n<h2>How to Use This Reviewer</h2>\n<p>This reviewer combines <strong>all four uploaded files</strong> into one study guide. Every section follows the same pattern: Topic Overview → Key Terms → Detailed Explanation → Types → Processes → Examples → Comparisons → Key Points.</p>\n<h4><strong>Tags used in this reviewer:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> = facts, formulas, and orders you should know by heart.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> = ideas you must be able to explain and trace by hand.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong> = easy-to-confuse points and common mistakes.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[TIP]</strong> = a memory trick.</li></ul>\n<h3>Files Covered</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Topic</th><th>Where in this reviewer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Week13_BinaryTrees_Terminologies_Traversal.pdf (41 slides)</td><td>Terminologies, types, representation, traversals, rebuilding trees</td><td>Part 1</td></tr><tr><td>Week14_BinaryTrees_Operations.pdf (40 slides)</td><td>Basic tree operations, binary search trees (search, insert, delete)</td><td>Part 2</td></tr><tr><td>Week15_BinaryTrees_Expressions_Efficiency.pdf (38 slides)</td><td>Expression trees, efficiency (Big O), balanced trees (AVL, red-black)</td><td>Part 3</td></tr><tr><td>aditional notes.pdf (4 pages)</td><td>Stack or Queue exercise (LIFO vs FIFO)</td><td>Part 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Note on sources:</strong> The slides also contain practice activities, takeaway questions, and exercises. As you asked, those are <strong>not</strong> turned into quizzes here. Where a slide shows a <strong>worked example</strong> (with its answer), it is included and explained.</p>\n<h1>PART 1: Terminologies, Types, Representation, and Traversal (Week 13)</h1>\n<h2>1.1 Topic Overview</h2>\n<p>A <strong>binary tree</strong> is a special kind of tree used to store data in a hierarchy (parent → child). It is the base of many important structures such as <strong>binary search trees (BST)</strong>, <strong>heaps</strong>, and <strong>expression trees</strong>. This part teaches you:</p>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">The words used to describe trees (root, leaf, depth, height…)</li><li value=\"2\">The types of binary trees (full, perfect, complete, skewed, balanced)</li><li value=\"3\">How to store a binary tree in Java (linked nodes and arrays)</li><li value=\"4\">How to visit every node (traversal): preorder, inorder, postorder, level order</li><li value=\"5\">How to rebuild a tree from its traversal results</li></ol>\n<h3>Quick Review: What Is a Tree?</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>tree</strong> is a <strong>non-linear data structure</strong> made of <strong>nodes</strong> connected by <strong>edges</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">It shows a <strong>hierarchy</strong> (parent to child), not a straight line like arrays or linked lists.</li><li class=\"lv1\">There is <strong>exactly one path between any two nodes</strong>, so there are <strong>no cycles</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Real-life trees: folders and files on a computer, a company org chart, the HTML of a web page (the DOM).</li></ul>\n<h3>What Makes a Tree \"Binary\"?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>binary tree</strong> is a tree where <strong>each node has at most two children</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">The two children are called the <strong>left child</strong> and the <strong>right child</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Left and right matter.</strong> A node with only a left child is a <strong>different tree</strong> from a node with only a right child.</li><li class=\"lv1\">A binary tree can also be <strong>empty</strong> (no nodes at all).</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>   A            A\n  /              \\\n B                B\n(B is left child)  (B is right child)</code></pre>\n<p>These are <strong>two DIFFERENT binary trees</strong>. <strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong></p>\n<h2>1.2 Key Terms and Definitions</h2>\n<p>The slides use this <strong>sample tree</strong> for most examples. Keep it in mind:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>     A\n   /   \\\n  B     C\n / \\     \\\nD   E     F\n   /\n  G</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>7 nodes:</strong> A, B, C, D, E, F, G</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>6 edges.</strong> A tree with <strong>n nodes always has n − 1 edges</strong>. <strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>Core Terms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>In the sample tree</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Node</strong></td><td>One element in the tree; holds data</td><td>A, B, C…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edge</strong></td><td>The link between a parent and a child</td><td>A–B, A–C…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Root</strong></td><td>The topmost node; it has <strong>no parent</strong></td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parent</strong></td><td>A node that has a child below it</td><td>B is parent of D and E</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Child</strong></td><td>A node directly below another node</td><td>D and E are children of B</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Siblings</strong></td><td>Nodes with the <strong>same parent</strong></td><td>D and E; B and C</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Leaf (external</strong> <strong>node)</strong></td><td>A node with <strong>no children</strong></td><td>D, G, F</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Internal node</strong></td><td>A node with <strong>at least one child</strong></td><td>A, B, C, E</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ancestor</strong></td><td>Any node on the path from the root to a node</td><td>Ancestors of G: E, B, A</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Descendant</strong></td><td>Any node below a given node</td><td>Descendants of B: D, E, G</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Subtree</strong></td><td>A node <strong>plus all its descendants</strong></td><td>B, D, E, G form a subtree</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Degree of a node</strong></td><td>Number of children it has</td><td>A = 2, C = 1, D = 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Path, Level, Depth, and Height</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Path:</strong> a sequence of nodes linked by edges (example: A → B → E → G).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Level:</strong> same as depth. The <strong>root is at level 0</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Depth of a node:</strong> number of <strong>edges from the root down to it</strong>. Depth(G) = 3.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Height of a node:</strong> number of <strong>edges on the longest path from it down to a leaf</strong>. Height(B) = 2.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Height of the tree</strong> = height of the root = 3 (in the sample tree).</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Level 0:        A\nLevel 1:      B   C\nLevel 2:     D E    F\nLevel 3:       G</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong> Some books count <strong>nodes</strong> instead of edges, which makes the tree height 4. <strong>In this class we count EDGES.</strong> Also, an <strong>empty tree has height −1</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Depth vs Height (Easy Way to Remember)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Depth looks UP:</strong> \"How far am I from the root?\"</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Height looks DOWN:</strong> \"How far is my farthest leaf?\"</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Depth</th><th>Height</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Every <strong>leaf has height 0</strong>. The <strong>root always has depth 0</strong>.</p>\n<h2>1.3 Types and Classifications of Binary Trees</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Definition</th><th>Quick example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Full (strict)</strong></td><td><strong>Every node has 0 or 2 children</strong> (never exactly 1)</td><td>A with B, C; B with D, E</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Perfect</strong></td><td><strong>All internal nodes have 2 children</strong> <strong>AND all leaves are on the same level</strong></td><td>A; B, C; D, E, F, G</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Complete</strong></td><td><strong>All levels are full except maybe the</strong> <strong>last</strong>, and the <strong>last level is filled from</strong> <strong>left to right</strong></td><td>A; B, C; D, E, F (F is the left child of C)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Skewed</strong> <strong>(degenerate)</strong></td><td><strong>Every node has only one child.</strong> It behaves like a <strong>linked list</strong></td><td>A → B → C → D (all right children)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Balanced</strong></td><td>For <strong>every node</strong>, the heights of the left and right subtrees differ by <strong>at most 1</strong></td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Full:        A          Perfect:       A          Complete:      A\n            / \\                       /   \\                      / \\\n           B   C                     B     C                    B   C\n          / \\                       / \\   / \\                  / \\ /\n         D   E                     D   E F   G                D  E F\n\nSkewed:  A\n          \\\n           B\n            \\\n             C\n              \\\n               D</code></pre>\n<h3>Is the Sample Tree Full? Complete? Perfect? Balanced?</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>C and E each have only one child</td></tr><tr><td>Perfect?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Leaves D, G, F are on different levels</td></tr><tr><td>Complete?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>C has a right child but <strong>no left child</strong>, so level 2 is not filled left to right</td></tr><tr><td>Balanced?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Every node's left/right height difference is at most 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>How the balanced check works</strong> (empty subtree height = −1):</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A: left height 2, right height 1 → difference 1 ✔</li><li class=\"lv1\">B: left 0, right 1 → difference 1 ✔</li><li class=\"lv1\">C: left −1, right 0 → difference 1 ✔</li><li class=\"lv1\">E: left 0, right −1 → difference 1 ✔</li></ul>\n<p>Every difference is at most 1, so the tree is balanced. Notice a tree can be <strong>balanced even</strong> <strong>if it is not full, complete, or perfect</strong>. <strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong></p>\n<h3>Comparison: Full vs Perfect vs Complete</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Full</th><th>Perfect</th><th>Complete</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nodes with exactly 1 child allowed?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (at most one node, on the last level)</td></tr><tr><td>All leaves on the same level?</td><td>Not required</td><td><strong>Required</strong></td><td>Not required</td></tr><tr><td>Last level filled left to right?</td><td>Not required</td><td>Yes (it is completely full)</td><td><strong>Required</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Relationship</td><td>Every perfect tree is full and complete</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>[TIP]</strong> Full = \"no lonely children.\" Perfect = \"a neat triangle, completely filled.\" Complete = \"no gaps, filled from the left.\" Skewed = \"a stick (a linked list).\"</p>\n<h2>1.4 Important Properties (Memorize These)</h2>\n<p>For a binary tree (height counted in <strong>edges</strong>):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Max nodes at level i</strong></td><td><strong>2^i</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Max nodes in a tree of height h</strong></td><td><strong>2^(h+1) − 1</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min height of a tree with n nodes</strong></td><td><strong>⌊log₂ n⌋</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Max height of a tree with n nodes</strong></td><td><strong>n − 1</strong> (skewed)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edges in a tree with n nodes</strong></td><td><strong>n − 1</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>In a full binary tree</strong></td><td><strong>leaves = internal nodes + 1</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Example from the slides:</strong> a perfect tree of height 3 has 2^(3+1) − 1 = 2^4 − 1 = <strong>15 nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why these work [UNDERSTAND]:</strong> Level 0 has 1 node, level 1 has up to 2, level 2 up to 4, level 3 up to 8, and so on (each level can double). Adding 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 gives 2^4 − 1. The <strong>tallest</strong> tree is the skewed one (one node per level, so height n − 1). The <strong>shortest</strong> tree packs nodes in as wide as possible, so its height grows slowly like log₂ n.</p>\n<h2>1.5 Representing a Binary Tree in Java</h2>\n<h3>Representation 1: Linked Nodes</h3>\n<p>Each node stores its <strong>data</strong> plus <strong>references to its left and right child</strong>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>class Node {\n    char data;\n    Node left;\n    Node right;\n\n    Node(char data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>missing child is simply</strong> <strong><code>null</code></strong> .</li><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>leaf has both</strong> <strong><code>left</code></strong> <strong>and</strong> <strong><code>right</code></strong> <strong>set to</strong> <strong><code>null</code></strong> .</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Building the sample tree:</strong></h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>Node root = new Node('A');\nroot.left = new Node('B');\nroot.right = new Node('C');\n\nroot.left.left = new Node('D');\nroot.left.right = new Node('E');\nroot.right.right = new Node('F');\n\nroot.left.right.left = new Node('G');</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How to trace it:</strong> <code>root.left.right.left</code> means start at A → go left to B → go right to E → go left to <strong>G</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Representation 2: Array</h3>\n<p>Store the nodes <strong>level by level</strong>. For a node at index <strong>i</strong> (0-based):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Find</th><th>Index formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Left child</strong></td><td><strong>2i + 1</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Right child</strong></td><td><strong>2i + 2</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Parent</strong></td><td><strong>(i − 1) / 2</strong> (integer division)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> tree A; B, C; D, E, F (with C's right slot empty) is stored as:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>index</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>6</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>value</td><td>A</td><td>B</td><td>C</td><td>D</td><td>E</td><td>F</td><td>–</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Check: left child of index 1 (B) = 2(1)+1 = 3 → D ✔. Right child of index 1 = 2(1)+2 = 4 → E ✔. Parent of index 5 (F) = (5−1)/2 = 2 → C ✔.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Best for complete trees</strong> (no wasted slots).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Bad for skewed trees</strong> (lots of empty slots).</li><li class=\"lv1\">This is how a <strong>heap</strong> (priority queue, Module 4) is stored.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comparison: Linked Nodes vs Array</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Linked nodes</th><th>Array</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How children are found</td><td><code>node.left</code> , <code>node.right</code> references</td><td>Formulas 2i+1 and 2i+2</td></tr><tr><td>Extra memory</td><td>Two references per node</td><td>No references needed</td></tr><tr><td>Good for</td><td>Any shape</td><td><strong>Complete</strong> trees (heaps)</td></tr><tr><td>Bad for</td><td>–</td><td><strong>Skewed</strong> trees (many empty slots)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>1.6 Tree Traversal</h2>\n<h3>Topic Overview</h3>\n<p><strong>Traversal</strong> means <strong>visiting every node exactly once in a fixed order.</strong> We need it to print all items, search for a value, count nodes, find the height, copy a tree, and evaluate expressions (Week 15). Arrays and linked lists have <strong>one</strong> natural order. <strong>Trees have several.</strong></p>\n<h3>Two Families of Traversal</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Idea</th><th>Traversals</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Depth-first</strong> <strong>(DFS)</strong></td><td>Go <strong>deep</strong> down one branch before moving to the next</td><td><strong>Preorder</strong> (Root, Left, Right), <strong>Inorder</strong> (Left, Root, Right), <strong>Postorder</strong> (Left, Right, Root)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Breadth-first</strong> <strong>(BFS)</strong></td><td>Go <strong>level by level</strong></td><td><strong>Level order</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>[TIP]</strong> The name tells you <strong>when the root is visited</strong>: <strong>pre = before</strong> its children, <strong>in = in</strong> <strong>between</strong>, <strong>post = after</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Results on the Sample Tree</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Traversal</th><th>Order</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Preorder</strong></td><td>Root → Left → Right</td><td><strong>A B D E G C F</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inorder</strong></td><td>Left → Root → Right</td><td><strong>D B G E A C F</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Postorder</strong></td><td>Left → Right → Root</td><td><strong>D G E B F C A</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Level order</strong></td><td>Level 0, 1, 2, 3…</td><td><strong>A B C D E F G</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Preorder (Root → Left → Right)</h3>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong> visit A, go left to B, visit B, go left to D, visit D, back to B, go right to E, visit E, go left to G, visit G, back up to A, go right to C, visit C, go right to F, visit F.</p>\n<p><strong>Result: A B D E G C F. Uses:</strong> making a <strong>copy</strong> of a tree, writing <strong>prefix</strong> expressions.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static void preorder(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) {\n        return;                          // base case: empty subtree\n    }\n    System.out.print(node.data + \" \");  // 1. visit root\n    preorder(node.left);                 // 2. go left\n    preorder(node.right);                // 3. go right\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Why recursion fits trees:</strong> every subtree is also a binary tree, so the same method works on each part.</p>\n<h3>Inorder (Left → Root → Right)</h3>\n<p>Go as far left as you can, visit, then the parent, then the right side.</p>\n<p><strong>Result: D B G E A C F. Use:</strong> in a <strong>binary search tree, inorder gives the values in SORTED</strong> <strong>order</strong> (Week 14).</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static void inorder(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);                  // 1. go left\n    System.out.print(node.data + \" \");  // 2. visit root\n    inorder(node.right);                 // 3. go right\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>Postorder (Left → Right → Root)</h3>\n<p>Finish <strong>both children before</strong> visiting the parent. <strong>The root is always last.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Result: D G E B F C A. Uses: deleting</strong> a tree (free children before parent), computing <strong>height or size</strong>, writing <strong>postfix</strong> expressions.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static void postorder(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    postorder(node.left);                // 1. go left\n    postorder(node.right);               // 2. go right\n    System.out.print(node.data + \" \");  // 3. visit root\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> Only the <strong>position of the print line</strong> changes between the three DFS methods.</p>\n<h3>The \"Flag\" Method (Trick for Tracing by Hand)</h3>\n<p>Draw a path around the tree, <strong>starting at the left of the root and hugging every node</strong>. Then:</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Preorder:</strong> list a node when you pass its <strong>left side</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Inorder:</strong> list a node when you pass <strong>under</strong> it.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Postorder:</strong> list a node when you pass its <strong>right side</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Example tree: 1 is the root, with children 2 and 3; 2 has children 4 and 5.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Preorder: <strong>1 2 4 5 3</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\">Inorder: <strong>4 2 5 1 3</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\">Postorder: <strong>4 5 2 3 1</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>Level Order (BFS) with a Queue</h3>\n<p>Visit nodes <strong>level by level, left to right.</strong> It uses a <strong>queue (first in, first out)</strong>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static void levelOrder(Node root) {\n    if (root == null) return;\n\n    Queue&lt;Node&gt; queue = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();\n    queue.add(root);\n\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        Node current = queue.poll();          // take from the front\n        System.out.print(current.data + \" \");\n\n        if (current.left != null) queue.add(current.left);\n        if (current.right != null) queue.add(current.right);\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<h4><strong>Queue trace for the sample tree:</strong></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Remove</th><th>Print</th><th>Queue after adding children</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>start</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>[A]</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>[B, C]</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>[C, D, E]</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>C</td><td>C</td><td>[D, E, F]</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>D</td><td>D</td><td>[E, F]</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>E</td><td>E</td><td>[F, G]</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>F</td><td>F</td><td>[G]</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>G</td><td>G</td><td>[] → stop</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Output: A B C D E F G. How to trace by hand:</strong> remove from the front, print it, add its left child then its right child to the back.</p>\n<h3>Inorder Without Recursion (Using a Stack)</h3>\n<p>Recursion uses the <strong>call stack</strong> behind the scenes (Module 3). You can use <strong>your own stack</strong> instead.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static void inorderIterative(Node root) {\n    Deque&lt;Node&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n    Node current = root;\n\n    while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {\n        while (current != null) {      // go as far left as possible\n            stack.push(current);\n            current = current.left;\n        }\n        current = stack.pop();         // visit\n        System.out.print(current.data + \" \");\n        current = current.right;       // then go right\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Steps in words:</strong> (1) push every node while going left, (2) pop one and visit it, (3) move to its right child and repeat.</p>\n<h3>Summary of Traversals</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Traversal</th><th>Order</th><th>Data structure used</th><th>Common use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preorder</td><td>Root, L, R</td><td>Call stack / stack</td><td>Copy a tree, <strong>prefix</strong> form</td></tr><tr><td>Inorder</td><td>L, Root, R</td><td>Call stack / stack</td><td><strong>Sorted output</strong> from a BST</td></tr><tr><td>Postorder</td><td>L, R, Root</td><td>Call stack / stack</td><td><strong>Delete</strong> tree, <strong>height</strong>, <strong>postfix</strong> form</td></tr><tr><td>Level order</td><td>Level by level</td><td><strong>Queue</strong></td><td>Shortest path by levels, printing by level</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All four visit each node once, so <strong>each takes O(n) time</strong>. <strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong></p>\n<h2>1.7 Rebuilding a Tree from Traversals</h2>\n<h4><strong>Can you rebuild a tree if you only know its traversal output?</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>One traversal is NOT enough</strong> (many trees share the same preorder).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Preorder + Inorder</strong> is enough (if all values are unique).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Postorder + Inorder</strong> is also enough.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Rule (Preorder + Inorder):</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">The <strong>first item in preorder is the root</strong>.</li><li value=\"2\"><strong>Find that root in the inorder list.</strong></li><li value=\"3\">Everything <strong>left</strong> of it in inorder is the <strong>left subtree</strong>; everything <strong>right</strong> is the <strong>right subtree</strong>.</li><li value=\"4\"><strong>Repeat</strong> for each subtree.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Preorder: A B D E C F · Inorder: D B E A F C</p>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Root = <strong>A</strong> (first in preorder).</li><li value=\"2\">Inorder splits at A: left = {D, B, E}, right = {F, C}.</li><li value=\"3\">Left part: next in preorder is <strong>B</strong> → splits {D} | {E}.</li><li value=\"4\">Right part: next in preorder is <strong>C</strong> → splits {F} | { } (F is C's left child).</li></ol>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>    A\n   / \\\n  B   C\n / \\ /\nD  E F</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong> Preorder alone (or inorder alone) does not give a unique tree. You need inorder plus one of the other two. For <strong>postorder + inorder</strong>, the same idea works, but the <strong>last</strong> item in postorder is the root (the root is visited last in postorder). <em>(This last sentence</em> <em>follows from the postorder rule; the slide only states that the combination is enough.)</em></p>\n<h3>Worked Example: Traversals of a BST-Shaped Tree</h3>\n<p>The Week 13 activity uses this tree (50 at the root; 30 and 70 below it; 20, 40, 60, 80 below those; 10 under 20; 65 under 60):</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>        50\n      /    \\\n    30      70\n   /  \\    /  \\\n 20   40  60   80\n /          \\\n10          65</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Preorder:</strong> 50 30 20 10 40 70 60 65 80</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Inorder:</strong> 10 20 30 40 50 60 65 70 80 → <strong>sorted!</strong> This is the clue that the tree is a binary search tree.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Postorder:</strong> 10 20 40 30 65 60 80 70 50</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Level order:</strong> 50 30 70 20 40 60 80 10 65</li></ul>\n<h2>1.8 Key Points to Remember (Part 1)</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Binary tree = each node has <strong>at most two children</strong> (left, right). Left and right are different.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> n nodes → <strong>n − 1 edges</strong>. Leaf height = 0. Root depth = 0. Empty tree height = −1. <strong>Count edges, not nodes.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Max nodes at level i = 2^i. Max nodes at height h = 2^(h+1) − 1. Min height = ⌊log₂ n⌋. Max height = n − 1. Full tree: leaves = internal + 1.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Array formulas: left = 2i+1, right = 2i+2, parent = (i−1)/2.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Preorder = <strong>R L R</strong>; Inorder = <strong>L R R</strong> (Left, Root, Right); Postorder = <strong>L R</strong> <strong>Root</strong>; Level order = <strong>queue</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> DFS traversals differ <strong>only in when the root is visited</strong>. Level order uses a <strong>queue</strong>; the others use the <strong>call stack</strong> (or your own stack).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> Preorder + inorder (unique values) rebuilds a unique tree.</li></ul>\n<h1>PART 2: Operations on Binary Trees and Binary Search Trees (Week 14)</h1>\n<h2>2.1 Topic Overview</h2>\n<p>This part teaches you how to <strong>do things</strong> with a binary tree: count nodes, find the height, search, insert, and delete. It then introduces the <strong>binary search tree (BST)</strong>, a binary tree with an ordering rule that makes searching fast. You should be able to write recursive methods, explain the <strong>BST property</strong>, <strong>simulate search, insert, and delete by hand</strong>, and use a BST to solve a simple search problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Warm-up (traversal drill from the slides).</strong> The slide shows a tree with 8 at the root; 3 and 10 below it; 1 and 6 under 3; 14 under 10; 4 and 7 under 6; 13 under 14. If you apply the Week 13 traversals, the <strong>inorder output is sorted</strong>. This is the key observation: <em>inorder on a BST</em> <em>gives sorted output.</em></p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>     8\n   /   \\\n  3     10\n / \\      \\\n1   6      14\n   / \\     /\n  4   7   13</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Preorder: 8 3 1 6 4 7 10 14 13</li><li class=\"lv1\">Inorder: 1 3 4 6 7 8 10 13 14 (sorted)</li><li class=\"lv1\">Postorder: 1 4 7 6 3 13 14 10 8</li></ul>\n<h2>2.2 Key Terms and Definitions</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Binary search tree (BST)</strong></td><td>A binary tree where, for every node, <strong>all values in</strong> <strong>the left subtree are smaller</strong> and <strong>all values in the</strong> <strong>right subtree are greater</strong>. (In this class, <strong>no</strong> <strong>duplicate values</strong>.)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>BST property</strong></td><td>left subtree &lt; node &lt; right subtree, for <strong>every</strong> node</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inorder successor</strong></td><td>The <strong>next bigger value</strong>; the <strong>smallest value in the</strong> <strong>right subtree</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inorder predecessor</strong></td><td>The <strong>largest value in the left subtree</strong> (alternative to the successor for deleting)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Base case</strong></td><td>The empty tree ( <code>node == null</code> ) that stops the recursion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-balancing tree</strong></td><td>A BST that fixes its own shape (covered in Part 3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>2.3 Basic Operations on ANY Binary Tree</h2>\n<h3>The Pattern Behind Most Tree Operations</h3>\n<p>Almost every tree method has the same shape:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static RESULT operation(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) {\n        return BASE_VALUE;               // empty tree\n    }\n    RESULT left = operation(node.left);\n    RESULT right = operation(node.right);\n    return COMBINE(node, left, right);\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> Ask yourself: <em>\"If I already knew the answer for my left and right subtrees,</em> <em>how do I get the answer for the whole tree?\"</em> This week's Node class stores <strong>integers</strong>:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>class Node {\n    int data;\n    Node left;\n    Node right;\n    Node(int data) { this.data = data; }   // left and right start as null\nautomatically\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>Operation 1: Size (count all nodes)</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static int size(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    return 1 + size(node.left) + size(node.right);\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Size = me (1) + all nodes on my left + all nodes on my right.</strong></p>\n<h3>Operation 2: Height</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static int height(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) {\n        return -1;   // empty tree has height -1 (we count edges)\n    }\n    int leftHeight = height(node.left);\n    int rightHeight = height(node.right);\n    return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);\n}</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>leaf</strong>: 1 + max(−1, −1) = <strong>0</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">This is a <strong>postorder pattern</strong>: children first, then the parent.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong> The base case returns <strong>−1</strong>, not 0, because we count <strong>edges</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Operation 3: Count Leaves and Sum of Values</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static int countLeaves(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    if (node.left == null &amp;&amp; node.right == null) {\n        return 1;                         // a leaf counts as 1\n    }\n    return countLeaves(node.left) + countLeaves(node.right);\n}\n\nstatic int sum(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    return node.data + sum(node.left) + sum(node.right);\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The slide also suggests writing <code>max(Node node)</code> that returns the largest value in <strong>any</strong> binary tree (same pattern: compare the node's value with the maximum of the left and right subtrees).</p>\n<h3>Operation 4: Search in a General Binary Tree</h3>\n<p>In a normal binary tree, values are in <strong>no particular order</strong>. In the worst case we must <strong>check</strong> <strong>every node</strong>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static boolean contains(Node node, int target) {\n    if (node == null) return false;\n    if (node.data == target) return true;\n    return contains(node.left, target) || contains(node.right, target);\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Cost: O(n)</strong>, the same as linear search in an array. We can do better <strong>with ordering</strong> (a BST).</p>\n<h3>Operation 5: Insert in a General Binary Tree</h3>\n<p>With no ordering rule, a common choice is to <strong>fill the first empty spot in level order</strong>. This <strong>keeps the tree complete</strong>.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>static Node insertLevelOrder(Node root, int value) {\n    Node newNode = new Node(value);\n    if (root == null) return newNode;\n\n    Queue&lt;Node&gt; queue = new LinkedList&lt;&gt;();\n    queue.add(root);\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        Node current = queue.poll();\n        if (current.left == null) { current.left = newNode; break; }\n        else queue.add(current.left);\n        if (current.right == null) { current.right = newNode; break; }\n        else queue.add(current.right);\n    }\n    return root;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Steps:</strong> (1) make the new node, (2) if the tree is empty, it is the root, (3) walk the tree in level order, (4) put the new node at the first missing left or right child you find.</p>\n<h2>2.4 Binary Search Trees (BST)</h2>\n<h3>The BST Property</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>       50\n     /    \\\n   30      70\n  /  \\    /  \\\n20   40  60   80</code></pre>\n<p>For <strong>every node</strong>: left subtree values are <strong>smaller</strong>, right subtree values are <strong>greater</strong>. This is <strong>binary search (Module 2) stored as a tree.</strong></p>\n<h3>BST or Not? (Worked Example)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Tree 1</th><th>Tree 2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Shape</td><td>10 at the root; 5 and 15 below; 2 and <strong>12</strong> under 5</td><td>10 at the root; 5 and 15 below; 2 and 7 under 5; 20 under 15</td></tr><tr><td>BST?</td><td><strong>NOT a BST</strong></td><td><strong>Is a BST</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Reason</td><td>12 sits in the <strong>left subtree of 10</strong>, but <strong>12 &gt;</strong> <strong>10</strong>. (It looks fine next to its parent 5, but it breaks the rule for the root.)</td><td>Every node follows the rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>[WATCH OUT] Checking only parent and child is NOT enough.</strong> The rule applies to the <strong>whole subtree</strong>.</p>\n<h3>BST Search</h3>\n<p><strong>Idea:</strong> at each node compare the target with the node's value.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Equal</strong> → found</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Smaller</strong> → go <strong>left</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Greater</strong> → go <strong>right</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Reached null</strong> → <strong>not found</strong></li></ul>\n<p>Each step throws away one whole subtree, just like binary search throws away half the array.</p>\n<h4><strong>Trace on the 50-30-70-20-40-60-80 tree:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Search 60:</strong> 60 &gt; 50 → right; 60 &lt; 70 → left; 60 == 60 → <strong>FOUND (3 comparisons)</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Search 45:</strong> 50 → left to 30 → right to 40 → right is null → <strong>not found (3 comparisons)</strong>.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>// Recursive\nboolean search(Node node, int key) {\n    if (node == null) return false;\n    if (key == node.data) return true;\n    if (key &lt; node.data) {\n        return search(node.left, key);\n    }\n    return search(node.right, key);\n}\n\n// Iterative\nboolean search(int key) {\n    Node current = root;\n    while (current != null) {\n        if (key == current.data) {\n            return true;\n        }\n        current = (key &lt; current.data) ? current.left : current.right;\n    }\n    return false;\n}</code></pre>\n<h3>BST Insert</h3>\n<p><strong>Idea:</strong> a new value is <strong>always added as a leaf</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Start at the root.</li><li value=\"2\">Compare and move left or right, <strong>just like search</strong>.</li><li value=\"3\">When you reach <code>null</code> , <strong>put the new node there</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>The tree keeps its BST property after every insert.</p>\n<h4><strong>Build step by step: insert 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">insert 50 → 50 is the root.</li><li value=\"2\">insert 30 → 30 &lt; 50, goes left of 50.</li><li value=\"3\">insert 70 → 70 &gt; 50, goes right of 50.</li><li value=\"4\">insert 20, 40 → under 30 (left and right). Insert 60, 80 → under 70 (left and right).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Where does 35 go?</strong> 35 &lt; 50 → left. 35 &gt; 30 → right. 35 &lt; 40 → <strong>left of 40.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>public void insert(int key) {\n    root = insert(root, key);\n}\n\nprivate Node insert(Node node, int key) {\n    if (node == null) {\n        return new Node(key);             // found the empty spot\n    }\n    if (key &lt; node.data) {\n        node.left = insert(node.left, key);\n    } else if (key &gt; node.data) {\n        node.right = insert(node.right, key);\n    }\n    // key == node.data: duplicate, do nothing\n    return node;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> The method <strong>returns the (possibly new) subtree root</strong>, so the <strong>parent re-</strong> <strong>links correctly</strong>. Duplicates are ignored in this class.</p>\n<h3>Insert Order Changes the Shape</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Insert order</th><th>Resulting shape</th><th>Height</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70</td><td>Perfect, bushy tree (40 at root; 20, 60; 10, 30, 50, 70)</td><td><strong>2</strong></td></tr><tr><td>10, 20, 30, 40, 50</td><td>A <strong>skewed</strong> chain to the right (looks like a linked list)</td><td><strong>4</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sorted input makes a BST look like a linked list.</strong> This matters for efficiency (Part 3).</p>\n<h2>2.5 Delete and Other BST Operations</h2>\n<h3>Find Minimum and Maximum</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Minimum:</strong> keep going <strong>left</strong> until there is no left child.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Maximum:</strong> keep going <strong>right</strong> until there is no right child.</li></ul>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>private Node findMin(Node node) {\n    while (node.left != null) node = node.left;\n    return node;\n}\nprivate Node findMax(Node node) {\n    while (node.right != null) node = node.right;\n    return node;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>[TIP]</strong> Min = <strong>leftmost</strong>. Max = <strong>rightmost</strong>.</p>\n<h3>BST Delete: Three Cases</h3>\n<p><strong>First, search for the node to delete.</strong> Then:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Node to delete has…</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>No children</strong> (leaf)</td><td><strong>Just remove it</strong> (parent points to <code>null</code> )</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>One child</strong></td><td><strong>Replace the node with its only child</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>Two children</strong></td><td>Replace its value with its <strong>inorder successor</strong> (smallest value in the right subtree), <strong>then delete</strong> <strong>the successor</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The inorder successor is the next bigger value. <strong>It never has a left child</strong>, so deleting it is always Case 1 or Case 2.</p>\n<h3>Delete Demo (starting tree: insert 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35)</h3>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>       50\n     /    \\\n   30      70\n  /  \\    /  \\\n20   40  60   80\n    /\n   35</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Case 1: Delete 20 (leaf).</strong> 30's left link becomes <code>null</code> . Nothing else changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Case 2: Delete 40 (one child).</strong> 40 has only one child (35). Link 30 directly to 35. The BST rule still holds because 35 &gt; 30.</p>\n<p><strong>Case 3: Delete 50 (two children).</strong></p>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Find the inorder successor: smallest value in 50's right subtree. Go right to 70, then left as far as possible → <strong>60</strong>.</li><li value=\"2\"><strong>Copy 60 into the root.</strong></li><li value=\"3\"><strong>Delete the old 60</strong> from the right subtree (it is a leaf, Case 1).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> inorder before = 30 35 50 60 70 80; after = 30 35 60 70 80. <strong>Still sorted.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>public void delete(int key) {\n    root = delete(root, key);\n}\n\nprivate Node delete(Node node, int key) {\n    if (node == null) return null;                   // key not found\n\n    if (key &lt; node.data) {\n        node.left = delete(node.left, key);\n    } else if (key &gt; node.data) {\n        node.right = delete(node.right, key);\n    } else {\n        // Case 1 and Case 2\n        if (node.left == null) return node.right;\n        if (node.right == null) return node.left;\n\n        // Case 3: two children\n        Node successor = findMin(node.right);\n        node.data = successor.data;\n        node.right = delete(node.right, successor.data);\n    }\n    return node;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Why does</strong> <strong><code>return node.right</code></strong> <strong>handle Case 1 too?</strong> If the node is a leaf, <code>node.left ==</code> <code>null</code> is true, so we return <code>node.right</code> , which is also <code>null</code> . The parent's link becomes <code>null</code> and the leaf is removed. <strong>One line covers both \"no children\" and \"only a right child.\"</strong> (The next line handles \"only a left child.\")</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative for Case 3:</strong> use the <strong>inorder predecessor</strong> (largest value in the left subtree). <strong>Both are correct.</strong></p>\n<h3>The Full BST Class and Testing</h3>\n<p>The class <code>BinarySearchTree</code> has a private <code>root</code> and these public methods: <code>insert</code> , <code>delete</code> , <code>search</code> (iterative), <code>min()</code> , <code>max()</code> (assume a non-empty tree), <code>height()</code> , and <code>printSorted()</code> (which calls inorder). Helper methods are private.</p>\n<p><strong>Test from the slides:</strong> insert 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><code>printSorted()</code> → <strong>20 30 35 40 50 60 70 80</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><code>search(60)</code> → <strong>true</strong>; <code>search(45)</code> → <strong>false</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\">After <code>delete(20)</code> (leaf), <code>delete(40)</code> (one child), <code>delete(50)</code> (two children): <code>printSorted()</code> → <strong>30 35 60 70 80</strong>, and <code>height()</code> → <strong>2</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2>2.6 Important Comparisons (Week 14)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>General binary tree</th><th>Binary search tree</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Search</strong></td><td>Visit all nodes: <strong>O(n)</strong></td><td>Follow one path: <strong>O(h)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Insert</strong></td><td>First empty spot (level order)</td><td>Always as a <strong>new leaf</strong>: <strong>O(h)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Delete</strong></td><td>Replace with the deepest node</td><td><strong>3 cases</strong>: <strong>O(h)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min / Max</strong></td><td>Check all nodes: <strong>O(n)</strong></td><td>Leftmost / rightmost: <strong>O(h)</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sorted output</strong></td><td>Need to sort</td><td><strong>Inorder traversal: O(n)</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>h = height of the tree. (The slides say \"replace with the deepest node\" for deleting in a</em> <em>general binary tree but do not explain it further.)</em></p>\n<h2>2.7 Application Example: BST for a Search Problem (Graded Task 5 Preparation)</h2>\n<p><strong>Scenario from the slides:</strong> the CITE office keeps student records by student number. Records arrive in this order: <strong>45, 25, 65, 15, 35, 55, 75, 30, 60</strong> (short numbers stand for full IDs). The slide activity asks you to insert them into an empty BST, compare the search cost for 60 with linear search, delete 25, 55, then 45, and write the final inorder traversal.</p>\n<h4><strong>Worked trace of this scenario:</strong></h4>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>       45\n     /    \\\n   25      65\n  /  \\    /  \\\n15   35  55   75\n     /     \\\n    30      60</code></pre>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Comparisons to find 60 in the BST:</strong> 45 → 65 → 55 → 60 = <strong>4 comparisons</strong>. A <strong>linear search</strong> on the arrival list reaches 60 as the <strong>9th</strong> item = <strong>9 comparisons</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Delete 25</strong> (two children, 15 and 35): the successor is the smallest value in the right subtree, 30. Copy 30 into that node and delete the old 30. <strong>Delete 55</strong> (one child, 60): link 65 directly to 60. <strong>Delete 45</strong> (two children): the successor is 60. Copy 60 to the root and delete the old 60.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Final inorder: 15 30 35 60 65 75</strong> (sorted).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Extension (Graded Task 5 preparation):</strong> change the node so each node holds a <strong>student</strong> <strong>number (key)</strong> and a <strong>name (value)</strong>. Nodes are ordered by the key.</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>class StudentNode {\n    int studentNo;\n    String name;\n    StudentNode left, right;\n\n    StudentNode(int studentNo, String name) {\n        this.studentNo = studentNo;\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The slide asks for a method <code>String findName(int studentNo)</code> (returns the name or \"Not found\"), printing all students sorted by student number, and a discussion of <strong>why a BST</strong> <strong>beats an unsorted array for this office, and when it does not</strong> (a BST loses its advantage when it becomes skewed, for example when keys arrive already sorted).</p>\n<h3>Where BSTs Show Up in Real Software</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Java</strong> <strong><code>TreeMap</code></strong> <strong>and</strong> <strong><code>TreeSet</code></strong> <strong>:</strong> sorted maps and sets built on a <strong>self-balancing BST (red-</strong> <strong>black tree)</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Database indexes: B-trees</strong>, a wider cousin of the BST, find rows fast.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Autocomplete and dictionaries:</strong> keep words in sorted order for range lookups.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Scheduling:</strong> find the next event after a given time (<strong>successor search</strong>).</li></ul>\n<h2>2.8 Key Points to Remember (Part 2)</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[UNDERSTAND]</strong> Most tree operations follow one pattern: <strong>handle null, solve left, solve</strong> <strong>right, combine.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> BST rule: <strong>left &lt; node &lt; right for every subtree</strong> (not just parent and child).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE]</strong> Search and insert follow <strong>one path</strong> from the root, so cost depends on <strong>height: O(h)</strong>. Insert always adds a <strong>leaf</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE] Delete has 3 cases:</strong> leaf (remove), one child (replace with child), two children (copy the <strong>inorder successor</strong>, then delete it).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[MEMORIZE] Inorder traversal of a BST gives sorted output.</strong> Min = leftmost, max = rightmost.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>[UNDERSTAND] Insert order decides the shape</strong> of the tree. Sorted input gives a skewed tree (height n − 1).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><strong>[WATCH OUT]</strong> Empty tree height = −1 in <code>height()</code> . A general binary tree search is O(n); a BST search is O(h).</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "notice": "Source check: this reviewer file lists Part 3 (Week 15) and Part 4 (Stack or Queue notes) in its contents, but the file itself ends after Part 2 (page 28 of 28). Nothing was added. Some test questions on Week 15 and the Stack/Queue exercise come from the original slide and notes files and are labeled with their source."}, "purpcom": {"file": "purpcomrev.pdf", "html": "<h1>Communication for Academic Purposes: Complete Study Reviewer</h1>\n<p><em>Prepared from your 9 uploaded files: six lesson PDFs (Communication forAcademic</em> <em>Purposes, Academic Writing Style, Types ofAcademic Paper, Genres of Professional</em> <em>Writing, Communication in the Workplace, Purposive Writing) plus Additional Notes and</em> <em>the two Quiz Notes files (Review Quizzes Mod 4–6 and graded results).</em></p>\n<h2>How to use this reviewer</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Quiz Focus</strong></td><td>The concept shows up in the quiz notes. Quiz items are turned into facts to memorize, not practice questions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>✔ Confirmed</strong></td><td>The graded quiz results (Quiz Notes part 2) marked this as the correct answer.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>◐ Inferred</strong></td><td>The quiz notes (part 1) show the item with no marked answer, so the answer here is matched to the lesson content. Verify with your instructor if unsure.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⚠ Flag</strong></td><td>An inconsistency, odd wording, gap, or a point found only in the quiz notes and not in the lessons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> Part 1 Communication forAcademic Purposes · Part 2 Academic Writing Style · Part 3 Types ofAcademic Papers · Part 4 Genres of Professional Writing · Part 5 Communication in the Workplace · Part 6 Purposive Writing · Part 7 Quick Review (glossary, summaries, comparison tables, checklist, source flags)</p>\n<h2>Part 1: Communication for Academic Purposes</h2>\n<h3>1.1 Aims of the lesson</h3>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Write using <strong>academic writing style</strong> and appropriate structure.</li><li value=\"2\"><strong>Quote, summarize, and paraphrase</strong> texts to avoid <strong>plagiarism</strong>.</li><li value=\"3\"><strong>Cite sources using APA.</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>1.2 Choosing a topic</h3>\n<p>Finding a topic is the first step in research writing. A good topic relates to your course, is interesting, or deals with social issues (Sygaco, 2018).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Characteristic of an ideal topic</th><th>Meaning in the lesson</th><th>Plain English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Timely</strong></td><td>Useful information today and tomorrow</td><td>Still relevant now and later</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Well-defined</strong></td><td>Narrowed to a very specific theme</td><td>Focused, not too broad</td></tr><tr><td><strong>High level of creativity</strong></td><td>Connected with other disciplines, which makes it interesting</td><td>A fresh angle that links fields</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory tip:</strong> T-W-C = Timely, Well-defined, Creative.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> “Topic is unique” is NOT one of the three characteristics (in the graded item, “high level of creativity” was wrongly chosen as the exception).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> “General and overused” is clearly NOT an ideal-topic characteristic (Mod 4).</li><li class=\"lv1\">⚠ The lesson explains “timely” using the word “timeless.” Study it as “useful today and tomorrow.”</li></ul>\n<h3>1.3 Making an academic paper</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">List the <strong>topic sentence</strong> first so you can merge other paragraphs later. First drafts often repeat ideas, so a <strong>central argument</strong> keeps the paper cohesive and direct.</li><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>thesis statement</strong> is the major element showing the <strong>assertion, message, or central</strong> <strong>idea</strong>. The remaining paragraphs support it, and it builds the paper into a cohesive whole (Bauman, 2013). Making a thesis and outline also helps you generate new details and ideas.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part of the paper</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Introduction</strong></td><td>Mentions the specific topic; frames the thesis or purpose statement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Background</strong></td><td>Review of related literature and historical perspective; shows how current research and your paper parallel each other or make significance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Body</strong> (the “hinge”)</td><td>Gives clear ideas, evidence, and major ideas that support the argument</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conclusion</strong></td><td>Knits all the main points together to answer the central argument</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐ (thesis statement):</strong> Features are that it states an opinion or attitude, contains the main idea, and includes subtopics or sub-points. It is NOT written as a question. (This fits the lesson’s “assertion, message, central idea.”)</li></ul>\n<h3>1.4 Taking notes</h3>\n<p>Take notes from materials you think will be useful, even with many sources. Like a carpenter designing a house from gathered materials, your notes shape the voice and tone of your paper and give you a precise record for deciding what to keep or delete (Bullock, 2006).</p>\n<h4><strong>Steps (as listed in the lesson):</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Write notes on an index card, notebook, or computer. Label every entry with <strong>author,</strong> <strong>title, and URL/pages</strong> so tracking sources is easy.</li><li value=\"2\">Use your own words through quoting (enclose in quotation marks).</li><li value=\"3\">Paraphrase or summarize the source text so readers can tell your own words from borrowed words.</li><li value=\"4\">Put a <strong>subject title</strong> on each note for quick identification.</li></ol>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">⚠ Step 2 is oddly worded. Quoting is word for word; “own words” means paraphrase or summary.</li></ul>\n<h3>1.5 Citing sources</h3>\n<p><strong>Citation</strong> (quiz definition): a formal reference to a published or unpublished source from which information is obtained while writing an academic paper.</p>\n<h4><strong>Why cite (3 reasons):</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">To <strong>acknowledge the authors</strong> of books, magazines, journals, interviews, and other sources.</li><li value=\"2\">To let readers <strong>verify</strong> your information.</li><li value=\"3\">To let readers <strong>replicate and expand</strong> your research.</li></ol>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> The statement that is NOT a reason: “Citing sources gives writers reasons to look for other useful information.”</li></ul>\n<p><strong>When to cite:</strong> when you quote exact words, paraphrase, or use ideas, data, or processes from any written source (books, articles, online material, unpublished documents).</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> Cite <strong>whenever you use information from an author or a reference</strong> <strong>source</strong>, not only if a teacher tells you to or when you have time.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔: Common knowledge</strong> is the type of information that does not have to be cited. Quotes, summaries, and paraphrases do. ⚠ “Common knowledge” is not discussed in the lessons.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Details to gather</strong> (Turabian, 2007): writer, editor, or translator; titles and subtitles; volume and edition numbers; page numbers; URL; published date; publisher; place of publication.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference list:</strong> arrange in <strong>alphabetical order</strong> using APA format.</p>\n<h3>1.6 Quoting</h3>\n<p><strong>Quoting</strong> keeps the writer’s statement <strong>word for word</strong>. It may be changed only by ellipses, omitting redundant details, or brackets. The only way to tell quoted material from your own words is <strong>quotation marks</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>MLA</th><th>APA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short quotation (lesson wording)</td><td>Four typed lines or fewer</td><td>Not more than forty words</td></tr><tr><td>Long (block) quotation</td><td>Five or more typed lines, indented one inch (or ten spaces) from the left margin</td><td>Lesson says quotes of not more than forty words are indented half an inch (five spaces)</td></tr><tr><td>Citation</td><td>Parenthetical citation at the end when no quotation marks are used</td><td>Same</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">⚠ The APA row is inconsistent in the lesson: “not more than forty words” is given for both short and indented quotes. In standard APA practice (outside your files), block quotes are for 40 words or more. Ask your instructor which version to follow.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Omission</td><td>Use <strong>ellipses</strong> (three dots)</td></tr><tr><td>Addition, change, or correction</td><td>Put it in <strong>brackets</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Periods and commas</td><td>Inside the quotation marks, except when a parenthetical citation ends the sentence: then the period goes <strong>after</strong> the parentheses</td></tr><tr><td>Question marks and exclamation points</td><td>Inside the quotation marks when followed by a parenthetical citation and then a period</td></tr><tr><td>Colons and semicolons</td><td><strong>Outside</strong> the quotation marks</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> The option that is NOT a rule to observe says periods or commas go <em>outside</em> quotation marks, with the period <em>before</em> the parenthesis. That is the reverse of the lesson rule above.</li></ul>\n<h3>1.7 Paraphrasing</h3>\n<p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> rewriting a statement, passage, or longer text so it does not look like the original but shares the <strong>same thought</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Informative paraphrase:</strong> takes the source’s tone, facts, and opinions as if they were the writer’s own.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Descriptive paraphrase:</strong> describes the source text rather than directly presenting its perspective or information (Veit et al., 1997).</li><li class=\"lv1\">A paraphrase must still be <strong>cited</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> Mod 4 asks you to pick the best paraphrase of a sentence about adoption being stressful in unique ways. The rule to apply: keep the same meaning, change the wording, and do not add or distort claims. (No answer is marked.)</li></ul>\n<h3>1.8 Summarizing</h3>\n<p><strong>Summary:</strong> your own words, immediately stating the <strong>main ideas</strong> of the source. It covers only the <strong>general aspect</strong> and does not go into details (Bullock, 2016).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Quote</th><th>Paraphrase</th><th>Summary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wording</td><td>Exact words of the author</td><td>Your own words</td><td>Your own words</td></tr><tr><td>Detail</td><td>Full</td><td>Full thought, same ideas</td><td>Main ideas only</td></tr><tr><td>Quotation marks</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Citation needed</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>1.9 Purpose, audience, and context (Suarez et al., 2018)</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose, audience, and context</strong> are important aspects of communication. They determine the <strong>content, language, mode, medium, and genre</strong> of what you write or deliver.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Same topic: trafficking of cultural property</th><th>Raise public awareness</th><th>Convince lawmakers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Audience and context</td><td>General public; smuggling is rampant</td><td>Lawmakers</td></tr><tr><td>Genre</td><td>Public service announcement (PSA)</td><td>Position paper</td></tr><tr><td>Language</td><td>Simple, vivid</td><td>Academic, formal</td></tr><tr><td>Content</td><td>General information (why taking artifacts is wrong) plus a clear call to action (report traffickers)</td><td>Importance of protecting cultural heritage and identity, with facts and figures</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> what does the writing task ask you to do?</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>What you must do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Inform</strong></td><td>Present accurate, objective data and information, possibly with your interpretation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Instruct / teach</strong></td><td>Explain how to go about a procedure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Entertain</strong></td><td>Include material that makes the audience laugh or identify with delightful situations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Persuade</strong></td><td>Use logical arguments and evidence, or moving (emotional) arguments</td></tr><tr><td>Academic requirement</td><td>Show your understanding of a concept discussed in class</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other purposes by paper type: a <strong>literary analysis</strong> shows skill in interpreting a text or understanding a technique or theory; an <strong>informative paper</strong> gives further understanding of a topic or how something works; a <strong>research report</strong> shows knowledge of the scientific process or identifies developments in the field; a <strong>proposal</strong> addresses a problem and suggests feasible solutions.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> “Does the writing task inform, instruct, teach, or entertain the audience?” points to <strong>Purpose</strong> (not audience, genre, or content).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Audience:</strong> For whom are you writing? Ask about their <strong>background</strong>, what they <strong>expect</strong> from you, and how much they <strong>already know</strong> about the topic. This shapes content, language, mode, and medium.</p>\n<p><strong>Context:</strong> Knowing purpose and audience helps you determine context. The clearer the context, the better your response.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A literary analysis for an essay contest honoring a National Artist follows the <strong>judges’</strong> <strong>criteria</strong>; the same paper as a course requirement follows the <strong>teacher’s grading rubric</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">An essay on caffeine addiction for a teen magazine on stress uses accessible, conversational language in print; the same topic in an international investigative report uses formal, objective language, and may be oral or visual (a documentary).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Genre:</strong> texts with a clear communication purpose meant for a specific audience and context. Readers and writers expect certain texts to follow a particular form or pattern.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">An <strong>information report</strong> (for example, a business presentation or technical report) gives information, not a position.</li><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>literary analysis</strong> argues a position for critics and readers of literature; a <strong>political</strong> <strong>analysis essay</strong> explains an issue for policy makers and lawmakers. Same family (argumentative), different structure and language.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Mode vs. medium (Kress, 2000):</strong></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Memory tip</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Mode</strong></td><td>The way meaning is represented: words, symbols, pictures</td><td>How meaning is <em>made</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Medium</strong></td><td>The way these are transmitted: print, audio, digital</td><td>How meaning is <em>carried</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Informative essays and technical papers are often accompanied by pictures, diagrams, or infographics and may be printed or electronic. If your instructor does not specify the mode and medium, find out how much leeway you have.</p>\n<p><strong>Stance:</strong> your <strong>attitude toward the topic</strong>. It shapes your <strong>tone</strong> and is set by the kind of writing and your purpose.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A technical report calls for an objective, analytical stance and a neutral tone.</li><li class=\"lv1\">A political analysis or policy proposal supports or critiques a policy or cause.</li><li class=\"lv1\">You may state your stance in the thesis, or withhold it if that persuades more effectively. Either way, it is conveyed through language and presentation.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 2: Academic Writing Style</h2>\n<h3>2.1 What is academic writing?</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Academic writing in English is the required standard in college writing. It is not used at all times in all settings, but it is the most appropriate style for much of what you write.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Its <strong>main purpose is to inform.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Factors to consider in writing (RAPF): R</strong>ole (who you are as the writer), <strong>A</strong>udience (intended reader), <strong>P</strong>urpose (reason for writing, such as showing steps in a recipe or explaining how a DVD player works), <strong>F</strong>ormat (form appropriate for your piece).</li></ul>\n<h3>2.2 Academic vs. non-academic writing</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Non-academic example</th><th>Why it is non-academic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Consumers liked on-line shopping because it’s quite convenient.</td><td>Simple and informal; uses a <strong>contraction</strong> (it’s)</td></tr><tr><td>If you own a business, the store space you rent or own is a major expense.</td><td><strong>Addresses the reader directly</strong> with second person <em>you</em></td></tr><tr><td>At first I thought that the people were talking about it? In fact, it is so boring.</td><td><strong>First person</strong> <em><strong>I</strong></em> (personal language); <strong>rhetorical question</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Academic example</th><th>Features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Convenience was cited by the consumers as the most important reason why they prefer on-line shopping.</td><td>Uses <strong>passive voice</strong></td></tr><tr><td>One factor that traditional retail business owners must consider is the cost of the store space.</td><td>Reader not addressed; <strong>no personal</strong> <strong>pronouns</strong>; formal tone and objective point of view</td></tr><tr><td>Hemingway’s use of deliberate ambiguity in the dialogue serves to heighten the reader’s interest.</td><td><strong>Impersonal, non-emotive</strong> language; no rhetorical question; objective third person even for personal opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">⚠ In the lesson’s “Why non-academic?” table the features of the academic versions (such as passive voice) appear in the same table. They describe the academic sentences.</li></ul>\n<h3>2.3 The four distinctive features (memory tip: FISH )</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Feature</th><th>One-line meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F</td><td><strong>Formality</strong></td><td>Formal vocabulary; no contractions or idioms</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td><strong>Impersonality</strong></td><td>No “you,” no rhetorical questions</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td><strong>Structured and specific</strong></td><td>Passive voice, noun forms, concrete language</td></tr><tr><td>H</td><td><strong>Hedging</strong></td><td>Cautious language</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4><strong>Formality</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Choose the more formal <strong>one-word verb</strong> over the two-word equivalent.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Do <strong>not</strong> use abbreviated forms and <strong>contractions</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Avoid <strong>idiomatic expressions</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Informal:</em> “If users know how search engines work they can deal better with them.” <em>Formal:</em> “An understanding of the fundamental operations of the search engine will provide improved user interface.”</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Impersonality</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Do not address readers directly with “you.”</li><li class=\"lv1\">Avoid asking rhetorical questions.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Personal</th><th>Impersonal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>If you don’t know how to navigate through an e-commerce site, you will waste a lot of time.</td><td>Navigating through an e-commerce site can be time-consuming for those who may be unfamiliar with the process.</td></tr><tr><td>The marketplace is growing so fast; pretty soon, you’d think the government would address the unresolved issues.</td><td>The rapid expansion of the marketplace is more than enough for the government to immediately address the unresolved issues.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4><strong>Structured and specific</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Choose <strong>passive construction</strong> to focus on the action.</li><li value=\"2\">Use <strong>noun forms instead of verbs</strong> (nominalization).</li><li value=\"3\">Use <strong>specific and concrete</strong> language.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Nominalization:</strong> changing verbs to nouns. It makes a sentence less active. Common endings: <strong>-tion, -ment, -ance</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Noun</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>solve</td><td>solution</td></tr><tr><td>perform</td><td>performance</td></tr><tr><td>accomplish</td><td>accomplishment</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Verb form:</em> “Further studies can be conducted to solve the technical problems.” <em>Nominalized:</em> “…to find solutions to the technical problems.”</li><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Verb form:</em> “The economy did not perform well.” <em>Nominalized:</em> “The performance of the economy was dismal.”</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Active vs. passive:</strong> Active: “The decision of the major petroleum-producing countries to cut production caused the sharp increase of oil prices.” Passive: “The sharp increase of the price of oil… was caused by the decision of the major petroleum-producing countries.”</p>\n<h4><strong>Specific vs. general; concrete vs. abstract:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">General “move” becomes specific “walk, jump, run, glide.”</li><li class=\"lv1\">Concrete words appeal to the senses: “beautiful” becomes “smooth skin, pointed nose, pearly teeth.”</li><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Vague:</em> “I will submit my resume at some future time.” <em>Specific:</em> “I will submit my resume on Monday.”</li><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Vague:</em> “The car is not in good condition.” <em>Specific:</em> “The car has no gasoline.”</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Hedging</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Hedging</strong> means using <strong>cautious language</strong> to temper the strength of a claim. Academic writing requires it even when you are quite certain of your data and conclusions.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>2 reasons for hedging:</strong> (1) to be <strong>objective and impersonal</strong>; (2) to provide <strong>greater</strong> <strong>interaction with readers</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><em>Unhedged:</em> “A repeat of terrorist attacks, as massive as 9/11, is just a matter of time.” <em>Hedged:</em> “It is possible that the threat of terrorism is more serious than ever before…” or “Terrorism experts suggest that global terrorism is still a serious threat…”</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hedging device</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Modal nouns</td><td>possibility of, probability of, certainty of, presumption of, likelihood of</td></tr><tr><td>Modal adjectives</td><td>possible, probable, certain</td></tr><tr><td>Modal (auxiliary) verbs</td><td>will, would, may, might, can, could, must, should</td></tr><tr><td>Other verbs</td><td>appear, assume, indicate, seem, suggest, tend</td></tr><tr><td><em>That</em> clause + combination</td><td>This indicates that…; These studies suggest that…; It seems probable that…; This would appear to be…; There might be a possibility…</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>2.4 Quiz Focus for Part 2</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>✔</strong> The statement NOT true of academic writing: “The language is informal, casual and may contain slang.” True statements: purpose, audience, and context matter; it needs a lot of preparation; it is clear, concise, and structured.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>✔ “Header”</strong> is NOT a distinctive feature of academic writing style (the four are formality, impersonality, structured and specific, hedging).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Sentence that follows academic standards (Mod 4): ✔</strong> the sentence “Mars is the focus of much scientific study and the foremost planet for human colonization” was graded wrong. The graded results do not show the correct choice. <strong>◐</strong> By the FISH features, “It is assumed that playing violent games may cause more aggression, bullying and fighting” fits best (impersonal passive plus hedging with <em>assumed</em> and <em>may</em>). The “Nurses must take into consideration…” sentence is also impersonal. The rhetorical question (“…so why do we still use them?”) breaks impersonality. ⚠ Confirm with your instructor.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 3: Types of Academic Papers</h2>\n<p>The lesson covers <strong>four types</strong>: the personal reflective essay, the literary analysis, the informative essay, and the problem-solution paper.</p>\n<h3>3.1 Overview</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Key feature</th><th>Voice / stance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Personal</strong> <strong>reflective</strong> <strong>essay</strong></td><td>Present your insights on an aspect of life as you observed and experienced it</td><td>Reflection on the <em>significance</em> of an experience, not just narration</td><td>Personal; first-person narrative from one who has learned something</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Literary</strong> <strong>analysis</strong></td><td>Show your understanding, evaluation, or appreciation of a literary work</td><td><strong>Arguable thesis</strong> <strong>statement</strong> and <strong>close</strong> <strong>reading</strong></td><td>Argues that your interpretation is valid, with textual evidence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Informative</strong> <strong>essay</strong></td><td>Provide information on topics unknown to most readers</td><td>Facts and evidence; reader leaves understanding the topic better</td><td>You are the expert; no opinion; straightforward but engaging</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Problem-</strong> <strong>solution paper</strong></td><td>Analyze a problem, then offer a solution</td><td>Clearly defined problem plus a feasible, supported solution</td><td>Objective, reasonable tone; persuades the reader it is the best option</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Before writing any paper, be clear about:</strong> purpose, audience and context, mode and medium, and stance (see Part 1).</p>\n<h3>3.2 The personal reflective essay</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Definition:</strong> presents your insights on a particular aspect of life as you have observed and experienced it. It can include vivid description of what you witnessed and went through. It is like inviting your audience into your mind as you narrate significant turning points and share your views.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Process of reflection:</strong> recall what happened in the past or look at your present circumstance, <strong>unveil what it means</strong> to you, and <strong>reveal what you realized</strong> because of it.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Goal:</strong> to enhance your perspective and inspire readers to explore their own thoughts about the subject.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Guide questions:</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Why do I consider this instance important?</li><li value=\"2\">How did I feel while it was happening?</li><li value=\"3\">Why did I react that way?</li><li value=\"4\">What lessons have I gained from it?</li><li value=\"5\">What have I learned about myself, other people, and the world?</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Subject:</strong> from daily incidents (bumping into an old high school friend on the way to class) to special episodes (helping a family in crisis). What matters is that it is a <strong>striking incident</strong> <strong>or turning point</strong> that made you pause and think.</p>\n<h4><strong>Features:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">You are not just narrating an event or describing a person or object. You <strong>reflect on its</strong> <strong>significance</strong>, which reveals the kind of person you have become.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Begins with an <strong>intriguing topic</strong> that interests you or stirs something in you.</li><li class=\"lv1\">The main body must interest the audience. It can be structured in various ways as long as ideas relate to one another and to the <strong>main insight</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Most common structure: a <strong>narrative</strong> told by one who has learned or realized something, with <strong>vivid, descriptive details</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">The “heart”: an expanded understanding of the world through your subject, an insight from pondering and probing.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Writing it:</strong> consider your purpose and communication context, audience, and mode and medium (these decide the level of language). <strong>Steps:</strong> 1) brainstorming, 2) writing the draft, 3) revising the draft, 4) presenting your work.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐ (purpose):</strong> the quiz offers “Reflect,” “enhance your perspective and inspire readers,” “inviting the audience into your mind” and “all of the above.” All three statements appear in the lesson, so “all of the choices” fits.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐ (statement NOT true of reflective essays):</strong> options were free-flowing; still an academic paper; uses factual material as well as personal experience; acceptable to use “I.” No answer is marked. The lesson supports the “academic paper,” “structured in various ways,” and first-person narrative points, but never says factual material is used. ⚠ Verify with your instructor.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐: Brainstorming</strong> is a step of the reflective essay (not the informative essay).</li></ul>\n<h3>3.3 The literary analysis</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Definition:</strong> written to show your <strong>understanding, evaluation, or appreciation</strong> of a literary work or an aspect of it. It may break the work into component parts (word choice, themes, motifs, other literary elements and devices) to understand <em>how a text</em> <em>means what it means</em>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">It is your <strong>interpretation</strong>, and the interpretation must have a <strong>basis in the text</strong>, which you provide as evidence.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Features:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">You argue that your interpretation is valid, so you need a clear, <strong>arguable thesis</strong> <strong>statement</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Pay attention to the language of the work and the patterns or themes that emerge. Do a <strong>close reading</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Close reading:</strong> examining the words used, understanding references to culture, history, or other texts, identifying the structure of the text, and other relevant features. These later become your <strong>evidence</strong> for the thesis.</li><li class=\"lv1\">In the body, keep returning to your thesis. You may <strong>summarize, paraphrase, or quote</strong> directly, but be judicious: quote only when necessary and explain why you chose the quoted material.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Cite</strong> quoted lines. Literary analyses usually follow <strong>MLA</strong>: cite the author’s <strong>surname and</strong> <strong>the page number</strong>. If the literary work is your only source, your professor may ask for the page number only.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Conclusion:</strong> summarize main points, restate your thesis, or focus on one major point to tie everything together.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Planning questions:</strong> Why are you writing it (understanding of elements, close reading practice, one text or comparing several)? Who is the audience and what do they already know? Is it a written paper or also a forum presentation? Do you give an objective interpretation or share how you feel about the work?</p>\n<p><strong>Steps (2):</strong> 1) reading the literary work, 2) writing the literary analysis paper.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> the feature of a literary analysis is an <strong>arguable thesis statement</strong> (not “problem,” “purposeful information,” or “intriguing topic”, which belong to other types).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔: Close reading</strong> is the term for examining words, references, and structure to build evidence.</li></ul>\n<h3>3.4 The informative essay</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Definition:</strong> written to provide information on topics <strong>not known to a vast majority of</strong> <strong>readers</strong>. You research, give facts and evidence, and act as the <strong>expert</strong>. The end goal is that the reader understands the topic better. Readers expect <strong>up-to-date information</strong> and <strong>explanations of complex terms</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Features:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Provides <strong>concrete and purposeful information</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Ideally <strong>no opinion</strong>; present information in a straightforward but engaging way.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Topic is <strong>clear and focused</strong>: broad enough to break into parts, narrow enough to avoid talking about everything related to it.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Uses <strong>facts about real people and events</strong> and evidence.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Uses different <strong>rhetorical patterns</strong>: define complex terms, explain a process, analyze causes and effects, define unique terminology, compare and contrast.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Steps:</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Write about an assigned topic from an interesting angle, or choose an engaging topic.</li><li value=\"2\">Generate ideas about it.</li><li value=\"3\">Begin research that first addresses your questions.</li><li value=\"4\">Write a tentative thesis statement that states what your topic is and what you will discuss.</li><li value=\"5\">Choose an organization based on the aspects of the topic you will discuss.</li><li value=\"6\">Begin with a <strong>“hook”</strong> (a statement that grabs attention).</li><li value=\"7\">In the body, keep referring to your thesis.</li><li value=\"8\">End clearly: summarize main points, identify implications, or pose a challenge to readers.</li></ol>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> the step belonging to the informative essay is “Write about an assigned topic from an interesting angle or choose an engaging topic.” Do not confuse it with “Reading the literary work” (literary analysis), “Brainstorming” (reflective essay), or “Determine the perspective in analyzing the problem” (problem-solution).</li></ul>\n<h3>3.5 The problem-solution paper</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Definition:</strong> analyzes a problem and then offers a solution. Your analysis gives a way of looking at a challenging problem and a basis for a feasible solution. Both analysis and solution must be supported by <strong>facts, details, or examples</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Features:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>clearly defined problem</strong>, including why it is a problem and why it needs to be addressed.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Explain your proposed solution and <strong>why it is the most feasible</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Your objective is to <strong>persuade</strong> the reader that you offer the <strong>best solution after</strong> <strong>weighing all the options</strong>, so adopt an <strong>objective, reasonable tone</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Writing it:</strong> make your purpose clear; identify who will read your proposal; your <strong>perspective</strong> in analyzing the problem and proposing a solution determines your <strong>stance</strong>; decide the <strong>mode of delivery</strong> (tables, diagrams, visuals).</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> “persuade your reader that you are offering the best alternative after weighing all the options” = <strong>problem-solution essay</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>3.6 Which paper is it? (comparison of clues)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue in the question</th><th>Paper type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Turning point, lessons learned about self and the world</td><td>Personal reflective essay</td></tr><tr><td>Arguable thesis, close reading, MLA citation of the text</td><td>Literary analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Hook, facts, research, no opinion, rhetorical patterns</td><td>Informative essay</td></tr><tr><td>Defined problem, feasible solution, best alternative</td><td>Problem-solution paper</td></tr><tr><td>Begins with an intriguing topic</td><td>Reflective essay</td></tr><tr><td>Gives purposeful information</td><td>Informative essay</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Part 4: Genres of Professional Writing</h2>\n<h3>4.1 Genres and conventions</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">There are different <strong>genres</strong> of professional documents. Each follows <strong>generic</strong> <strong>conventions</strong>: conventions for the <strong>formatting, layout, and contents</strong> of the document.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Business letters and memos</strong> are two of the most common forms of professional correspondence (<strong>✔ Quiz Focus</strong>: memos and business letters, not reports or minutes).</li></ul>\n<h3>4.2 Letter vs. memo (Pfeiffer, 2013)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Business letter</strong></th><th><strong>Memo</strong> (memorandum)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>Conveys information to a member of one organization from someone <strong>outside</strong> that organization</td><td>Written by a member of an organization to one or more members of the <strong>same</strong> organization</td></tr><tr><td>Communication type</td><td><strong>External</strong></td><td><strong>Internal</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Special parts</td><td>Inside address; signature block; salutation; complimentary close</td><td>Immediately gives <strong>date,</strong> <strong>addressee, sender, subject</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Not needed</td><td></td><td>Salutation, complimentary close, signature block</td></tr><tr><td>Uses</td><td>Apply for a job, complain, order supplies, make a sales pitch</td><td>Official announcement, directive, guideline, or reminder to colleagues within or across departments</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>4.3 The business letter</h3>\n<p><strong>Salutation:</strong> “Dear” + title (Mr., Ms., Dr., Prof.) + last name + <strong>colon</strong>. <strong>Quiz Focus ◐:</strong> the right form is “Dear Dr. Santos:” (colon, not comma; surname, not full name).</p>\n<p><strong>Complimentary close:</strong> Sincerely, Respectfully, Best regards, Kind regards, Yours truly. Keep it neutral and check what the organization prefers. Do not use endearing words unless you have an intimate relationship with the recipient; stay formal to the end.</p>\n<h4><strong>Sample format (top to bottom):</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Company letterhead (then two or more blank spaces)</li><li value=\"2\">Date of letter (two or more blank spaces)</li><li value=\"3\">Recipient’s name, position/designation, address (one blank line)</li><li value=\"4\">Salutation: Dear + courtesy title + reader’s last name:</li><li value=\"5\">Paragraphs, <strong>single-spaced</strong>, with <strong>one blank line</strong> between paragraphs</li><li value=\"6\">Complimentary close (three blank lines for the signature)</li><li value=\"7\">Sender’s name and title/designation</li></ol>\n<h4><strong>Parts of a business letter (Additional Notes):</strong></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it contains</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heading</strong></td><td>Complete address of the sender (often on customized company paper)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Date</strong></td><td>Spell out the month, then day and year</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Inside address</strong></td><td><strong>Three lines</strong>: recipient’s complete name; position; complete address of the company</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Salutation</strong></td><td>Abbreviation of the person’s designation + surname + colon</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Introduction</strong></td><td>States the <strong>purpose</strong> of the letter at the start</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Body</strong></td><td><strong>Supporting details</strong>; may use several paragraphs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Conclusion</strong></td><td>Your <strong>appeal or call for action</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Complimentary close</strong></td><td>Matches your relationship with the recipient; stay formal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Signature line</strong></td><td>Full name on the first line, designation on the next</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔:</strong> inside address = <strong>3 lines</strong>; supporting details = <strong>body</strong>; appeal for action = <strong>conclusion</strong>. <strong>◐</strong> purpose = <strong>introduction</strong>; the part that addresses the recipient = <strong>salutation</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Quiz Focus ✔ (format settings):</strong> single-line spacing, a blank (“double”) line between paragraphs, <strong>no indent</strong>. In Word, line spacing and indentation are set on the <strong>Paragraph</strong> group of the Ribbon, not with Enter, Spacebar, or Tab. ⚠ “Double lines between paragraphs” means one blank line between single-spaced paragraphs, matching the template above.</li></ul>\n<h3>4.4 The memo</h3>\n<p><strong>Uses (Marsen, 2013):</strong> formally document a verbal exchange; inform, notify, or update colleagues; request, remind, or recommend; introduce specific documents.</p>\n<h4><strong>Memo template:</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">About two inches of blank space, then the heading <strong>Memorandum</strong> or <strong>Memo</strong></li><li value=\"2\"><strong>To:</strong> readers’ names and designations (individual or group)</li><li value=\"3\"><strong>From:</strong> writer’s name and designation</li><li value=\"4\"><strong>Date:</strong> current date</li><li value=\"5\"><strong>Subject:</strong> what the memo is about</li><li value=\"6\"><strong>Opening paragraph:</strong> grabs attention; introduces the purpose, context and problem, and the specific assignment or task</li><li value=\"7\"><strong>Body paragraph:</strong> explains purpose and context in more detail; uses lists if needed</li><li value=\"8\"><strong>Closing paragraph:</strong> request for action, deadline, and reasons; ends courteously</li></ol>\n<h3>4.5 Mechanics and style guides</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Know the <strong>mechanics</strong>: punctuation, capitalization, numbers vs. figures, abbreviations. Many organizations have their own <strong>style guides</strong>; find out which one a company follows.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Spell out acronyms at first mention</strong>, then use the acronym (De La Salle Lipa (DLSL) … DLSL students).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Spell out symbols</strong> such as the ampersand (“and,” not “&amp;”).</li><li class=\"lv1\">In general, <strong>spell out numbers one to nine</strong> as words; write the rest as figures.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Resources named: <strong>Associated Press (AP) Stylebook</strong> and Pearson’s “Mechanics of Writing.”</li></ul>\n<h3>4.6 Design principles of professional writing</h3>\n<p>Good design supports the purposes of professional writing: persuading readers to act and recording information so key points are easy to see.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Paper:</strong> size (A4, short bond, US legal), type (board, bond, craft), quality (substance 20 or 24; 70 or 80 gsm).</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Negative (white) space</strong> lets the audience engage with content in an orderly, comfortable way.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Arrangement:</strong> the order of paragraphs <em>and</em> the layout on the page.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Font size, font choice, effects</strong> (bold, italics) and <strong>line breaks</strong> highlight key information.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Legibility:</strong> how easy it is to read and tell one letterform from another.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Basic fonts such as <strong>Times New Roman, Arial, Calibri, and Verdana</strong> are best for business letters.</li></ul>\n<h3>4.7 Business meetings, notes, and minutes</h3>\n<p>Meetings help employees communicate within the institution and get tasks done; goals and plans are placed before team members, who comment and share ideas.</p>\n<h4><strong>7 steps for a successful meeting (Balle, n.d.):</strong></h4>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Call the meeting to order on schedule; introduce new workers or guests; latecomers catch up during or after</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Pass an <strong>agenda</strong> so co-workers are guided step by step</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Give a <strong>summary of the previous meeting</strong> as the secretary reads the undertakings</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>End on time</strong>; the agenda lists all points; time each segment to avoid chit-chat</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ask the secretary to take the <strong>minutes</strong> (decisions, documentation, review at the next meeting)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Prohibit insults</strong> when the issue gets heated; resolve conflict separately</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>End with <strong>optimism</strong>; assign tasks and issues for the next meeting</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Basic meeting notes template fields:</strong> name of work group or project; date; local; agency; attendees; absentees; discussions; announcements (if any); decisions made (if any); items for follow-up; name of the person taking the minutes.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Traditional minutes</strong></th><th><strong>Modern minutes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Header</td><td>Date; name of recorder; agency</td><td>Project name; location; date; attendees; absentees</td></tr><tr><td>Organization</td><td><strong>Old business</strong> (carried over) and <strong>new</strong> <strong>business</strong>, listed as points then detailed</td><td><strong>By agenda item</strong> (Item #1, #2, …)</td></tr><tr><td>Detail</td><td>Detailed account of discussion with names of everyone who contributed or presented</td><td>Name of presenter; what was discussed and decided</td></tr><tr><td>Next steps</td><td>Date, time, location and agenda of next meeting; attendees who must gather information or present; <strong>adjournment time</strong></td><td><strong>Next steps</strong>: specific task, <strong>person</strong> <strong>responsible</strong>, specific <strong>deadline</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Distributing minutes:</strong> send to everyone on the team or committee as a reminder of what was discussed, decided, and the next steps. If a participant sees an <strong>error</strong>, contact the preparer to revise. If an item was <strong>overlooked</strong>, tell the preparer so it can be added. An updated version is then distributed to all attendees.</p>\n<h3>4.8 Writing an effective workplace email</h3>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Identify your recipients and their positions.</li><li value=\"2\">Give a <strong>formal yet warm greeting</strong> (for example, “I hope you are well”).</li><li value=\"3\">State the <strong>purpose</strong> of your correspondence.</li><li value=\"4\">You may end with a thank-you and your complete name and department.</li><li value=\"5\"><strong>Review</strong> before sending: accuracy of information, appropriate tone, word choice, and language register.</li><li value=\"6\">Check whether you need to send an <strong>attachment</strong>, to avoid sending an <strong>erratum</strong> (a mistake in content or attachment).</li></ol>\n<p>(The 11 detailed email tips from Additional Notes are in Part 6.)</p>", "notice": "Source check: this reviewer file lists Parts 5-7 (Communication in the Workplace, Purposive Writing, Quick Review) in its contents, but the file itself ends at section 4.8 (page 22 of 22). Nothing was added. Test questions on those topics come from the supplied lesson and quiz-note files and are labeled with their source."}, "inforas": {"file": "inforasreviewer.pdf", "html": "<h1>Network and System Security – Lesson 1 Reviewer</h1>\n<p><em>Built from the two files in your ZIP: (1) “Lesson 1: Network and System Security” (40 slides,</em> <em>Parts 1–9) and (2) “Lesson 1: SSDLC &amp; OWASP &amp; SQL Injection” (13 slides, labeled Module 5,</em> <em>Topics 1–4).</em></p>\n<p><strong>How to read this:</strong> Everything not marked otherwise comes from the slides. Lines marked <strong>Added explanation</strong> are my clarifications to help you understand; they are not written on the slides. <strong>Memory cue</strong> lines are the slides’ own memory aids.</p>\n<h1>FILE 1 — Network and System Security</h1>\n<h2>Learning Objectives</h2>\n<p>By the end of the lesson you should be able to:</p>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Explain network security controls</li><li value=\"2\">Compare firewalls, IDS, IPS, and proxies</li><li value=\"3\">Describe segmentation and secure architecture</li><li value=\"4\">Apply OS hardening basics</li><li value=\"5\">Compare DAC, MAC, RBAC, and ABAC</li><li value=\"6\">Explain IAM, authentication, authorization, and MFA/2FA</li><li value=\"7\">Identify wireless security concepts</li><li value=\"8\">Differentiate malware types</li><li value=\"9\">Recognize common attacks and defenses</li></ol>\n<p><em>Course context: Information Assurance / Cybersecurity, for 2nd-year IT students. Topics:</em> <em>network security, access control, IAM, wireless security, malware, and common attacks.</em></p>\n<h2>The Big Picture: Layers of Defense</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Network security</strong> protects <strong>devices, users, applications, data, and network traffic</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Different controls work together as <strong>layers of defense</strong>. The lesson’s closing message: <strong>Cybersecurity is Defense in Depth</strong>, meaning multiple controls work together to protect systems, users, networks, and data.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>No single security control is enough.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\">Basic flow: <strong>User / Device → Security Controls (Firewall, IDS, IPS, Proxy) →</strong> <strong>Applications / Data.</strong></li></ul>\n<h2>Part 1 — Network Security Fundamentals</h2>\n<h3>Firewall = Gate</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> A firewall controls network traffic based on <strong>security rules</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> Device → Firewall → <strong>checks rules</strong> → <strong>ALLOW or BLOCK</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A university firewall allows students to access the school website but <strong>blocks</strong> <strong>direct access to an internal database</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Firewall = Gate.</em></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Added explanation:</strong> Think of a guard at a gate holding a list of who may pass. Traffic that matches an allow rule gets in; everything else is stopped.</li></ul>\n<h3>IDS — Intrusion Detection System = CCTV / Detect</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> An IDS <strong>detects suspicious activity and sends an alert</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> Network traffic → IDS → suspicious activity → <strong>ALERT</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> An IDS notices <strong>one computer scanning hundreds of university computers</strong> and alerts the security team.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>IDS = CCTV / Detect.</em> A camera records and warns, but it does not stop the intruder by itself.</li></ul>\n<h3>IPS — Intrusion Prevention System = CCTV + Guard</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> An IPS <strong>detects suspicious activity and can automatically block it</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> Traffic → IPS → attack detected → <strong>BLOCK</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> An IPS detects a <strong>known malicious attack pattern</strong> and blocks the connection.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>IPS = CCTV + Guard.</em></li></ul>\n<h3>IDS vs IPS</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>IDS</th><th>IPS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Detects threats</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Sends alerts</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Automatically blocks</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Main purpose</td><td><strong>Detection</strong></td><td><strong>Prevention</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>IDS = Detect. IPS = Detect + Block.</strong></p>\n<h3>Proxy = Middleman</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> A proxy <strong>sits between the user and the Internet</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> User → Proxy → Internet.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A university routes student web requests through a proxy that <strong>blocks</strong> <strong>prohibited websites</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Proxy = Middleman.</em></li></ul>\n<h3>Comparing the four network controls</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Role</th><th>Memory cue</th><th>Slide example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Firewall</strong></td><td>Controls traffic using security rules (allow/block)</td><td>Gate</td><td>Students reach the school website; direct access to the internal database is blocked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IDS</strong></td><td>Detects suspicious activity and alerts</td><td>CCTV / Detect</td><td>Alert when one computer scans hundreds of computers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IPS</strong></td><td>Detects and automatically blocks</td><td>CCTV + Guard</td><td>Blocks a known malicious attack pattern</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Proxy</strong></td><td>Sits between user and Internet</td><td>Middleman</td><td>Blocks prohibited websites for students</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Part 2 — Secure Network Architecture</h2>\n<h3>Network Segmentation = Separate</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> Dividing <strong>one large network into smaller separate networks</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> e.g., Student Network, Faculty Network, Finance Network, and Server Network kept apart.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> If one area is compromised, <strong>attackers have fewer systems they can</strong> <strong>reach</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Segmentation = Separate.</em></li></ul>\n<h3>DMZ — Demilitarized Zone</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> A <strong>separate network area for public-facing systems</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> Internet → Firewall → <strong>DMZ</strong> → Internal Network.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> The <strong>public university website → DMZ</strong>; <strong>student records/database → Internal</strong> <strong>Network</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>DMZ = public systems separated.</em></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Added explanation:</strong> Public servers must be reachable from the Internet, so they are the most exposed. Putting them in a DMZ means that if one is broken into, the attacker is still not inside the internal network.</li></ul>\n<h3>Least Privilege = Only what is needed</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> Give users and systems <strong>only the access they need</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> User → requested resource → <strong>access check</strong> → ALLOW / DENY.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example (student):</strong> ✓ Internet, ✓ LMS, ✗ Payroll database, ✗ Administrator settings.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Added explanation:</strong> LMS = Learning Management System.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 3 — Operating System Security</h2>\n<h3>OS Hardening = Reduce Attack Surface</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Hardening</strong> means securing an operating system by <strong>removing unnecessary risks</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Goal:</strong> fewer weak points for attackers.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Common hardening steps:</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Install security patches</li><li value=\"2\">Remove unnecessary software</li><li value=\"3\">Disable unnecessary services</li><li value=\"4\">Use strong authentication</li><li value=\"5\">Limit administrator access</li><li value=\"6\">Enable firewall/security tools</li><li value=\"7\">Enable logging</li></ol>\n<h3>Patch and Attack Surface</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Patch:</strong> fixes bugs and security vulnerabilities.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Attack surface: all possible entry points</strong> into a system.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Patch process:</strong> Vulnerability found → Patch released → System updated → <strong>Risk</strong> <strong>reduced</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Attack-surface examples:</strong> open ports, unnecessary services, outdated software, weak passwords, unused accounts.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Patch closes doors.</em></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Added explanation:</strong> Each item in the examples list is a “door.” Hardening closes or removes as many doors as possible, so there are fewer places to attack.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 4 — Access Control Models</h2>\n<p>Access control answers: <strong>“Who is allowed to access what?”</strong></p>\n<h3>DAC — Discretionary Access Control (Owner decides)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">The <strong>owner of a resource decides</strong> who gets access.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: File owner → gives permission → user.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A <strong>Google Drive</strong> file owner decides who can view or edit the file.</li></ul>\n<h3>MAC — Mandatory Access Control (Security rules decide)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Access is controlled by <strong>security classifications and system rules</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Security label → user clearance → access decision.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example: Confidential</strong> file + user <strong>without clearance = DENIED</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>RBAC — Role-Based Access Control (Role decides)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Access is based on the user’s <strong>job role</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: User → Role → Permissions.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> Registrar staff → student records; Finance staff → financial records; IT admin → system administration.</li></ul>\n<h3>ABAC — Attribute-Based Access Control (Conditions decide)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Access is based on <strong>attributes or conditions</strong> such as <strong>role, device, location, time, and</strong> <strong>resource</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: User + Resource + Conditions → access decision.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> Admin + company laptop + work hours → <strong>ALLOW</strong>. Admin + unknown device → <strong>DENY</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comparison of the four models</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Main basis</th><th>Simple memory</th><th>Slide example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>DAC</strong></td><td>Owner</td><td>Owner decides</td><td>Google Drive file owner shares the file</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MAC</strong></td><td>Security classification</td><td>Rules decide</td><td>Confidential file, no clearance = denied</td></tr><tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>Job role</td><td>Role decides</td><td>Registrar → student records</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ABAC</strong></td><td>Attributes / conditions</td><td>Conditions decide</td><td>Admin allowed only on company laptop during work hours</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4><strong>Added explanation (easy-to-confuse pairs):</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>RBAC vs ABAC:</strong> Both can involve a role, but RBAC stops at the role. ABAC looks at the role <strong>plus</strong> other conditions (device, location, time). In the ABAC example, the same admin role gets different results depending on the device.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>DAC vs MAC:</strong> In DAC a person (the owner) chooses; in MAC the system’s security labels and rules decide, regardless of what an individual would like.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 5 — Identity and Access Management (IAM)</h2>\n<h3>IAM — Identity and Access Management</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">IAM manages <strong>who users are, how they prove identity, what they can access, and</strong> <strong>when access is removed</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Lifecycle: <strong>Create identity → Authenticate → Authorize → Monitor.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> When an employee joins, an account is created and permissions are assigned. When they leave, <strong>access is removed</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Manage identities + access.</em></li></ul>\n<h3>Authentication vs Authorization</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Authentication</th><th>Authorization</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question</td><td><strong>“Who are you?”</strong></td><td><strong>“What can you access?”</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Deals with</td><td>Identity</td><td>Permissions</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Username + password</td><td>Portal allowed; payroll admin denied</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>MFA / 2FA</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>MFA (Multi-FactorAuthentication):</strong> uses <strong>2 or more</strong> authentication factors.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>2FA (Two-FactorAuthentication):</strong> uses <strong>exactly 2</strong> factors.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>The three factor types:</strong></li><li class=\"lv2\">Something you <strong>KNOW</strong></li><li class=\"lv2\">Something you <strong>HAVE</strong></li><li class=\"lv2\">Something you <strong>ARE</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> Password + OTP → 2FA.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Why it works:</strong> Even if a password is stolen, the attacker <strong>still needs another factor</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>More than one proof.</em></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Added explanation:</strong> OTP = One-Time Password. It is typically something you have (e.g., a code on your phone). Two different <em>types</em> of factor are what count, so a password plus a second password would still be only one type.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 6 — Wireless Network Security</h2>\n<h3>Wireless basics</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>SSID:</strong> the <strong>name of a Wi-Fi network</strong>, such as <em>University-WiFi</em>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>WPA2 / WPA3:</strong> Wi-Fi <strong>security standards</strong> for <strong>authentication and encryption</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Wi-Fi encryption:</strong> protects wireless communication from being <strong>easily read</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h4><strong>Basic defenses:</strong></h4>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Use trusted networks.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Prefer WPA2/WPA3.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Avoid suspicious public Wi-Fi.</li></ul>\n<h3>Rogue / Fake Access Point</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>What it is:</strong> An <strong>unauthorized or fake Wi-Fi access point</strong> that may <strong>imitate a legitimate</strong> <strong>network</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>How it works:</strong> User → Fake Wi-Fi → Attacker.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> Legitimate network: <em>University-WiFi</em>. The attacker creates <em>University_Free_WiFi</em>, and the user connects to the fake one.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Memory cue:</strong> <em>Fake Wi-Fi can trick users.</em></li></ul>\n<h2>Part 7 — Malware</h2>\n<p><strong>Malware = Malicious Software.</strong> Its goals can include: damaging systems, stealing information, disrupting operations, gaining unauthorized access, and encrypting or locking data. The four types covered are <strong>Virus, Worm, Trojan, and Ransomware</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Virus — needs a host/file</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Malware that <strong>attaches itself to another file or program</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Infected file → user runs file → virus executes → other files infected.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A user opens an infected document and the malware spreads to other files.</li></ul>\n<h3>Worm — spreads by itself</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Malware that can <strong>spread automatically across networks</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Infected computer → finds vulnerable computer → spreads → more computers.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A worm uses a network weakness to spread <strong>without the user opening each</strong> <strong>infected file</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Trojan — fake / disguised</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Malware <strong>disguised as legitimate software or a useful file</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Fake useful app → user installs → malware runs.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> “Free GCash Reward App.apk” looks useful but is actually malware.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ransomware — encrypt/lock + demand</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Malware that <strong>encrypts or locks data and demands payment</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Malware enters → files encrypted → access lost → ransom demand.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Defenses:</strong> backups, patching, endpoint protection, email filtering, least privilege, user awareness.</li></ul>\n<h3>Malware comparison</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Malware</th><th>Main behavior</th><th>Memory cue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Virus</strong></td><td>Attaches to files</td><td>Needs a host</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Worm</strong></td><td>Spreads automatically</td><td>Spreads itself</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trojan</strong></td><td>Pretends to be legitimate</td><td>Fake</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ransomware</strong></td><td>Locks/encrypts data</td><td>Encrypt + Demand</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Key differences:</strong> A virus needs a user to run an infected file; a worm spreads on its own through network weaknesses; a trojan tricks the user into installing it; ransomware is defined by its <em>effect</em> (locked data + payment demand).</p>\n<h2>Part 8 — Common Cyberattacks</h2>\n<h3>Social Engineering — trick the person</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Manipulating people instead of directly attacking technology.</strong></li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Attacker gains trust → victim is manipulated → information/access is given.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> Someone pretends to be <strong>IT support</strong> and asks an employee for a <strong>verification</strong> <strong>code</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Phishing is one type of social engineering.</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>Phishing — fake message</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">A <strong>fake message designed to trick a person</strong> into giving information or performing an action.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: Fake email/SMS → victim clicks → fake website → credentials stolen.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A fake GCash message: “Your account will be suspended. Click here to verify.”</li></ul>\n<h3>DoS vs DDoS</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>DoS</th><th>DDoS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>Denial-of-Service</td><td>Distributed Denial-of-Service</td></tr><tr><td>Goal</td><td>Make a service unavailable</td><td>Same goal, using many compromised devices to overwhelm the target</td></tr><tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Attacker → Target</td><td>Many devices → Target</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>One/main source</td><td><strong>Many</strong> sources</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>MITM — Man-in-the-Middle (attacker in the middle)</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">An attacker gets <strong>between two communicating parties</strong> and may <strong>observe or</strong> <strong>manipulate</strong> the communication.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Flow: User → Attacker → Website.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example:</strong> A user connects to an unsafe fake public Wi-Fi network.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Defenses:</strong> HTTPS/TLS, trusted Wi-Fi, VPN when appropriate, certificate validation.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Connection:</strong> A rogue access point (Part 6) is one way a MITM position can be created.</li></ul>\n<h2>Part 9 — Putting Everything Together</h2>\n<h3>University cybersecurity scenario</h3>\n<p>Traffic path: <strong>Internet → Firewall → DMZ → Internal Network</strong>, with separate Student, Faculty, Finance, and Server networks. Each control has a job:</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>IDS</strong> monitors traffic</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>IPS</strong> blocks attacks</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Proxy</strong> filters web access</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>IAM</strong> controls user access</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>MFA</strong> protects accounts</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>OS hardening</strong> protects servers</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>WPA2/WPA3</strong> protects wireless networks</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Segmentation</strong> separates departments</li></ul>\n<h3>Which control fits which situation?</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unauthorized Internet traffic tries to access an internal server</td><td><strong>Firewall</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A security team needs an alert when suspicious scanning is detected</td><td><strong>IDS</strong></td></tr><tr><td>An attack should be automatically blocked</td><td><strong>IPS</strong></td></tr><tr><td>The university wants to separate student and finance networks</td><td><strong>Network segmentation</strong></td></tr><tr><td>An employee must use a password and OTP</td><td><strong>MFA</strong></td></tr><tr><td>A fake Wi-Fi network imitates the university Wi-Fi</td><td><strong>Rogue access point</strong> (the threat)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Defense in layers (top to bottom on the slide)</h3>\n<p>UserAwareness → MFA → Access Control → OS Hardening → Firewall → IDS/IPS → Network Segmentation, with <strong>Backups</strong> as the last layer. <em>No single security control is</em> <em>enough.</em></p>\n<h3>Final cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>One-line meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Firewall</td><td>Controls traffic</td></tr><tr><td>IDS</td><td>Detects</td></tr><tr><td>IPS</td><td>Detects + blocks</td></tr><tr><td>Proxy</td><td>Middleman</td></tr><tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Separates networks</td></tr><tr><td>DMZ</td><td>Public servers separated</td></tr><tr><td>Least Privilege</td><td>Only necessary access</td></tr><tr><td>Hardening</td><td>Reduces attack surface</td></tr><tr><td>DAC</td><td>Owner decides</td></tr><tr><td>MAC</td><td>Security rules decide</td></tr><tr><td>RBAC</td><td>Role decides</td></tr><tr><td>ABAC</td><td>Conditions decide</td></tr><tr><td>IAM</td><td>Identity + access</td></tr><tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Who are you?</td></tr><tr><td>Authorization</td><td>What can you access?</td></tr><tr><td>MFA</td><td>Multiple factors</td></tr><tr><td>WPA2/WPA3</td><td>Wi-Fi security</td></tr><tr><td>Virus</td><td>Attaches to files</td></tr><tr><td>Worm</td><td>Spreads automatically</td></tr><tr><td>Trojan</td><td>Pretends to be legitimate</td></tr><tr><td>Ransomware</td><td>Encrypts/locks + demands payment</td></tr><tr><td>Phishing</td><td>Fake message</td></tr><tr><td>Social Engineering</td><td>Tricks people</td></tr><tr><td>DoS/DDoS</td><td>Service unavailable</td></tr><tr><td>MITM</td><td>Attacker in the middle</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">Protect the network → Firewall, IDS, IPS, Proxy</li><li class=\"lv1\">Separate systems → Segmentation, DMZ</li><li class=\"lv1\">Secure devices → OS hardening and patching</li><li class=\"lv1\">Control access → DAC, MAC, RBAC, ABAC</li><li class=\"lv1\">Protect identities → IAM, Authentication, MFA</li><li class=\"lv1\">Secure wireless → WPA2/WPA3, avoid rogue APs</li><li class=\"lv1\">Recognize malware → Virus, Worm, Trojan, Ransomware</li><li class=\"lv1\">Recognize attacks → Phishing, Social Engineering, DoS/DDoS, MITM</li></ul>\n<h1>FILE 2 — Secure Software Development: SSDLC, Vulnerabilities, OWASP, SQL Injection</h1>\n<p><em>Application security. The four topics are meant to be connected, not memorized as</em> <em>isolated definitions.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Lesson flow:</strong> SSDLC (security in every stage; <em>when</em> to build security in) → Vulnerability (a weakness; what we try to reduce) → OWASP Top 10 (risk awareness guide; <em>what</em> risks to look for) → SQL Injection (unsafe input problem; a database attack example).</p>\n<p><strong>Four goals:</strong> Plan security early · Recognize weaknesses · Use OWASP guidance · Protect database input.</p>\n<h2>Topic 1 — SSDLC (Secure Software Development Life Cycle)</h2>\n<p><strong>Normal SDLC + Security at every stage = SSDLC.</strong></p>\n<p>The six stages: <strong>Plan → Design → Develop → Test → Deploy → Maintain.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>SSDLC = secure thinking from start to finish.</strong></p>\n<h3>SSDLC process: the Online School Portal example</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Stage</th><th>Security activity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Planning</strong></td><td>Security requirements</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Design</strong></td><td>Protection architecture</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Development</strong></td><td>Secure coding</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Testing</strong></td><td>Controls verified</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>Secure configuration</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Maintenance</strong></td><td>Patch + monitor</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Core question:</strong> <em>What must be protected?</em></li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Mindset:</strong> Security <strong>before launch — and after</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Requirements vs Design (key distinction)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Requirements</th><th>Design</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asks</td><td><strong>WHAT</strong> security do we need?</td><td><strong>HOW</strong> will we implement it?</td></tr><tr><td>Examples (school portal)</td><td>Passwords; own records only; teacher access levels; personal data protected</td><td>Role-based accounts; secure password storage; data encryption; restricted database access</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4><strong>Planning asks “what?” — design answers “how?”</strong></h4>\n<p><strong>Added explanation:</strong> Notice how the examples line up. “Teacher access levels” (requirement) becomes “role-based accounts” (design); “personal data protected” becomes “data encryption” and “restricted database access.”</p>\n<h2>Topic 2 — Vulnerability</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Analogy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vulnerability</strong></td><td>A <strong>weakness</strong></td><td>Unlocked window</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Exploit</strong></td><td><strong>Using</strong> the weakness</td><td>Someone enters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Impact</strong></td><td>The <strong>consequence</strong></td><td>Items stolen</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Example chain:</strong> outdated plugin → attacker uses the flaw → customer data exposed.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Important point:</strong> A vulnerability can exist <strong>even before anyone attacks it</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\">Vulnerabilities are what we try to <strong>reduce</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2>Topic 3 — OWASP (Open Worldwide Application Security Project)</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>OWASP Top 10</strong> = a <strong>security checklist / awareness guide for major web application</strong> <strong>risks</strong>.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>OWASP gives guidance — developers still implement the protections.</strong></li></ul>\n<h4><strong>The ten risks shown on the slide:</strong></h4>\n<ol><li value=\"1\">Broken Access Control</li><li value=\"2\">Security Misconfiguration</li><li value=\"3\">Software Supply Chain Failures</li><li value=\"4\">Cryptographic Failures</li><li value=\"5\">Injection</li><li value=\"6\">Authentication Failures</li><li value=\"7\">Software &amp; Data Integrity Failures</li><li value=\"8\">Security Logging and Alerting Failures</li><li value=\"9\">Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions</li><li value=\"10\">Insecure Design</li></ol>\n<p><em>Note: the slide shows these as a graphic with no ranking or version year, so the numbering</em> <em>above is only for counting. Do not treat it as the official order.</em></p>\n<h3>SSDLC vs OWASP</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>SSDLC</th><th>OWASP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Answers</td><td><strong>WHEN</strong> security is considered</td><td><strong>WHAT</strong> risks to look for</td></tr><tr><td>Contents</td><td>Planning, Design, Coding, Testing, Deployment, Maintenance</td><td>Access control, Injection, Authentication, Misconfiguration, Monitoring failures</td></tr><tr><td>Nature</td><td><strong>The process</strong></td><td><strong>Guidance</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>SSDLC is the process; OWASP is guidance.</strong></p>\n<h2>Topic 4 — SQL Injection</h2>\n<p><strong>Setup:</strong> User → Website/App → Database. The app receives input and asks the database for information.</p>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Normal input</strong> → expected query → expected result.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Malicious input</strong> → <strong>altered query</strong> → unintended result.</li><li class=\"lv1\"><strong>Main mistake: the program trusts user input too much.</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>Example from the diagram (UNION-based)</h3>\n<p>The slide shows an attacker typing this into a form:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users--</code></pre>\n<p>The app builds this query:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\"><code>SELECT name, description FROM products WHERE category = 'Gifts' UNION SELECT\nusername, password FROM users--</code></pre>\n<p>The database returns <strong>all usernames and all passwords</strong> to the attacker instead of just products.</p>\n<p><strong>Added explanation (how the payload works):</strong> The leading <code>'</code> closes the quote around the category value. <code>UNION SELECT ...</code> attaches a second query that pulls data from a different table. The trailing <code>--</code> turns the rest of the original query into a comment so it is ignored. The slide itself shows only the example and result, not this step-by-step breakdown.</p>\n<h3>SQL injection defenses</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Defense</th><th>Role</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Parameterized</strong> <strong>queries</strong></td><td><strong>Main defense</strong></td><td>Keep the command and the input <strong>separate</strong></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Input validation</strong></td><td>Helpful filter</td><td>Reject wrong formats</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Least privilege</strong></td><td>Damage control</td><td>Limit the database account’s power</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Validation helps — but it does not replace parameterized queries.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Added explanation:</strong> With parameterized queries the database treats user input purely as data, never as part of the command, so injected text cannot change the query. Least privilege does not stop the attack, but limits how much damage a successful one can do (the same principle from File 1, Part 2).</p>\n<h2>Recap: What to Remember</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>In one line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SSDLC</strong></td><td>Security throughout software development</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vulnerability</strong></td><td>A weakness that could be exploited</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OWASP Top 10</strong></td><td>Guide to major web application risks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SQL Injection</strong></td><td>Unsafe input changes a database query</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>How they connect:</strong> SSDLC prevents weaknesses → OWASP helps identify risks → SQL injection shows why unsafe input matters.</p>\n<h2>Notes on the Source Files</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"lv1\">The two files use different numbering (File 1 uses Parts 1–9; File 2 is labeled Module 5 with Topics 1–4). I kept each file’s own structure so you can compare slide by slide.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>The slides do not give code for a parameterized query, the OWASP Top 10 version/ranking, or a definition of “input validation” beyond “reject wrong formats.” I did not invent these. Both files also contain a knowledge-check slide and a pair activity. As you requested, they are left out, but every concept they rely on is covered above.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "notice": ""}, "cworld": {"file": "cowrldreviewer.docx", "html": "<p>The Contemporary World | Modules 4–6 | Topics 14–24</p>\n<p>Prepared from the PDFs in the uploaded ZIP archive. This is a detailed study guide with source-page references. Explanations marked as “study note” clarify the lesson in simpler language; examples not stated in a source are illustrative, not quotations from the PDF.</p>\n<h2>Files successfully accessed and topics</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Filename</th>\n<th>Pages</th>\n<th>Topic / contents</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M4T14-2.pdf</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>Module 4, Topic 14: Development of International Financial Institutions and Economic Organizations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>M4T15.pdf</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Module 4, Topic 15: History of Global Market Integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M4T16.pdf</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Module 4, Topic 16: Global Corporations and the Role of Global Cities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>M5T17.pdf</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Module 5, Topic 17: Global Governance in the 21st Century</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M5T18-1.pdf</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Module 5, Topic 18: Effects of Globalization to Governments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>M5T19.pdf</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Module 5, Topic 19: The Relevance of the State amid Globalization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M5T20.pdf</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Module 5, Topic 20: Institutions that Govern International Relations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>M6T21.pdf</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Module 6, Topic 21: Global Citizenship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M6T22-1.pdf</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Module 6, Topic 22: Informationalism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>M6T23.pdf</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>Module 6, Topic 23: The Rise of Artificial Intelligence (AI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>M6T24.pdf</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Module 6, Topic 24: Ethical and Social Issues Related to AI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>aditional notes.pdf</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Supplementary notes: global governance, NGOs, global social movements, state amid globalization, disease spread, international relations, global citizenship, cyberactivism, informationalism, AI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Cyberactivism.pdf</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Cyberactivism definition and Global Huaren example / multiple futures of globalization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Coverage note: all 13 PDFs were opened and their text was extracted. The guide follows the module/topic order in the filenames. Some historical membership counts and current-event statements in the source files may be dated or time-sensitive; where useful, they are flagged rather than silently treated as timeless facts.</p>\n<h1>HOW TO USE THIS REVIEWER</h1>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Study the MUST MEMORIZE items first, then explain each UNDERSTAND THIS idea in your own words.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use the comparison tables to separate concepts that sound similar.</p></li>\n<li><p>Page references point to the PDF filename and printed PDF page. If a slide contains a broad topic, check the neighboring pages too.</p></li>\n<li><p>When an example is marked illustrative, use it to understand the concept, not as a claim that the original PDF used that exact example.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h1>MODULE 4 — GLOBAL MARKET INTEGRATION</h1>\n<h2>Topic 14: Development of International Financial Institutions and Economic Organizations</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M4T14-2.pdf, pp. 1–33</em></p>\n<h3>A. Lesson overview</h3>\n<p>This lesson explains how national markets become connected, why countries need international financial institutions, and how economic organizations coordinate trade, finance, and development. It traces the post-World War II financial order and introduces global and regional economic groupings.</p>\n<p><strong>MUST MEMORIZE —</strong> Global market integration links different countries’ markets so goods, services, money, technology, and information can move across borders more easily. International financial institutions (IFIs) support finance, development, and financial stability.</p>\n<h3>B. Important terms</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning in simple English</th>\n<th>Example / cue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global market integration</td>\n<td>Connecting countries’ markets so they function more like one larger market.</td>\n<td>A Philippine business buys materials abroad and sells products overseas.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Market integration</td>\n<td>Linking separate markets; prices and trading conditions may become more similar.</td>\n<td>A product sold in multiple countries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Economy</td>\n<td>The social system that organizes production, consumption, and trade.</td>\n<td>Workers produce goods, stores sell them, households consume them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Primary sector</td>\n<td>Extracts raw materials from nature.</td>\n<td>Fishing, farming, mining.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Secondary sector</td>\n<td>Transforms raw materials into manufactured goods.</td>\n<td>Turning mined metal into machinery.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Tertiary sector</td>\n<td>Provides services rather than physical goods.</td>\n<td>Transport, education, banking, healthcare.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International financial institution (IFI)</td>\n<td>An institution that provides financing, advice, or stability support across countries.</td>\n<td>IMF, World Bank, ADB.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Bretton Woods System</td>\n<td>Postwar international monetary framework agreed in 1944 to support stability and cooperation.</td>\n<td>It led to the IMF and IBRD/World Bank.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Trade liberalization</td>\n<td>Reducing barriers that restrict international trade.</td>\n<td>Lower tariffs under a trade agreement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Economic organization / bloc</td>\n<td>Countries cooperating on trade, investment, development, or economic stability.</td>\n<td>ASEAN, EU, G7, G20.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Neoliberalism (as presented in the lesson)</td>\n<td>An approach emphasizing markets, competition, and reducing barriers to trade.</td>\n<td>WTO-related arguments for lowering trade barriers.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. Global market integration and its benefits</h3>\n<p>Global market integration means markets are increasingly connected. A product may use materials from one country, be assembled in another, receive investment from a third, and be sold globally. Integration is supported by transport, communications, finance, trade agreements, and international organizations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Increases competition among producers.</p></li>\n<li><p>Can reduce price differences across markets.</p></li>\n<li><p>Gives producers access to larger customer bases.</p></li>\n<li><p>Gives consumers more choices and may lower prices.</p></li>\n<li><p>Can improve how goods and resources are distributed.</p></li>\n<li><p>Creates interdependence: disruptions in one location can affect suppliers and consumers elsewhere.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>UNDERSTAND THIS —</strong> Integration does not mean every country becomes identical or that everyone benefits equally. It means markets become more connected; the distribution of benefits depends on conditions, policies, and bargaining power.</p>\n<h3>D. International financial institutions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Institution</th>\n<th>Main role from the lesson</th>\n<th>Easy distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International Monetary Fund (IMF)</td>\n<td>Promotes international monetary/financial stability and assists countries facing financial difficulties. The lesson describes it as a lender of last resort.</td>\n<td>Focus: financial stability and short-/medium-term crisis support.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>World Bank / IBRD</td>\n<td>Funds longer-term development projects, including infrastructure, education, healthcare, poverty reduction, and economic development.</td>\n<td>Focus: development projects and poverty reduction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Asian Development Bank (ADB)</td>\n<td>Supports economic and social development, particularly in Asia and the Pacific.</td>\n<td>Regional development focus.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lesson explains that IFIs can help developing countries finance sustainable, climate-resilient, and low-carbon development. For example, a development loan may support roads or ports that make trade easier. (M4T14-2.pdf, pp. 8–17)</p>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> The IMF and World Bank are related historically but are not interchangeable: the IMF emphasizes monetary and financial stability; the World Bank emphasizes longer-term development and poverty reduction.</p>\n<h3>E. Bretton Woods System</h3>\n<p>In 1944, representatives met in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, after the disruption of two world wars. They sought a more stable international economic system and greater cooperation. The conference led to the IMF and the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD), which became part of the World Bank Group.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>The system linked currencies to a gold-based framework, with the US dollar playing a central role.</p></li>\n<li><p>Participating monetary authorities maintained exchange rates within agreed limits.</p></li>\n<li><p>The IMF was intended to oversee the system and support monetary stability.</p></li>\n<li><p>The framework encouraged fewer restrictions on international currency transactions and trade.</p></li>\n<li><p>The original Bretton Woods exchange-rate system later ended, but the institutions created in this period continued to influence global finance.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The PDF lists GATT (1947) as an early multilateral trade arrangement. After the Uruguay Round negotiations (1986–1993), the World Trade Organization (WTO) was established in 1995. The WTO deals with trade in goods and services and provides a forum for trade rules and disputes. The source also notes criticism that developing countries may struggle to counter advantages held by developed countries, especially in agriculture. (M4T14-2.pdf, pp. 10–17)</p>\n<h3>F. Major economic organizations and groupings</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Organization / grouping</th>\n<th>Main purpose / role</th>\n<th>Memory cue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>WTO — World Trade Organization</td>\n<td>Administers international trade rules and provides a setting for trade negotiations and disputes.</td>\n<td>Trade rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>OECD — Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development</td>\n<td>Forum for policy comparison, shared problems, good practices, and coordination among member economies.</td>\n<td>Policy learning among member economies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>OPEC — Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries</td>\n<td>Oil-producing members coordinate petroleum policies.</td>\n<td>Oil policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>EU — European Union</td>\n<td>Regional political and economic integration; cooperation on trade, laws, security, and policies.</td>\n<td>European integration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>NAFTA — North American Free Trade Agreement</td>\n<td>Historic trade pact among Canada, Mexico, and the United States, effective 1994; later replaced by USMCA in 2020.</td>\n<td>North American trade; historical name in PDF.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>G7</td>\n<td>Forum of major advanced economies: Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, the UK, and the US; the EU participates.</td>\n<td>Small advanced-economy forum.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>G20</td>\n<td>Forum including major advanced and emerging economies; leaders’ meetings became central during the 2008 financial crisis.</td>\n<td>Broader major-economy forum.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>BRICS</td>\n<td>Grouping originally known as BRIC (Brazil, Russia, India, China); South Africa joined in 2010.</td>\n<td>Major emerging economies; membership may evolve.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>ASEAN — Association of Southeast Asian Nations</td>\n<td>Regional cooperation across Southeast Asia, including economic, political, social, and cultural cooperation.</td>\n<td>Southeast Asia; Philippines is a member.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>African Union (AU)</td>\n<td>Continental organization promoting African unity, peace and security, development, governance, health, and climate cooperation.</td>\n<td>Pan-African cooperation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>SOURCE CAUTION —</strong> The PDF contains historical membership counts for the OECD, EU, NAFTA, and other groupings. Some counts and memberships can change over time. For an exam based on the supplied slides, know the role and the facts emphasized by your instructor; do not assume every dated count is current.</p>\n<h3>G. Key takeaway</h3>\n<p>Global market integration creates opportunities for trade and development, but it also creates interdependence. IFIs and economic organizations provide financing, rules, policy coordination, and forums for cooperation—while debates remain about fairness and whose interests the system serves.</p>\n<h2>Topic 15: History of Global Market Integration</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M4T15.pdf, pp. 1–10</em></p>\n<h3>A. Lesson overview</h3>\n<p>The lesson follows the historical development of cross-border connections, from early trade routes to modern digital globalization. It also introduces capitalism and socialism as competing economic models associated with industrial society.</p>\n<h3>B. Historical stages</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Main development</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1. Early trade and trade routes</td>\n<td>Routes such as the Silk Road and maritime routes connected regions exchanging silk, spices, metals, and agricultural products.</td>\n<td>Long-distance exchange existed before modern globalization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2. Age of Exploration (15th–17th centuries)</td>\n<td>Sea routes connected Europe, Asia, Africa, and the Americas more extensively; goods, people, resources, and ideas moved across regions.</td>\n<td>Expanded the geographic reach of international trade.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3. Industrial Revolution (18th–19th centuries)</td>\n<td>Steamships, railways, factories, and mass production made manufacturing and transportation faster and cheaper.</td>\n<td>More goods could be produced and sold internationally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4. Late 19th–early 20th century</td>\n<td>Trade, investment, banks, and cross-border business expanded. World War I and the Great Depression disrupted connections.</td>\n<td>Shows that integration can grow and then be disrupted by crisis and conflict.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5. Bretton Woods System (1944 onward)</td>\n<td>Postwar cooperation produced institutions such as the IMF and World Bank.</td>\n<td>Built institutions to support stability and reconstruction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6. Growth of international trade (1950s–1980s)</td>\n<td>Trade agreements and economic organizations helped reduce barriers and expand trade.</td>\n<td>Trade became a larger part of many national economies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7. Modern globalization (1990s–present in the source)</td>\n<td>Internet and digital technology, cheaper transport, multinational corporations, foreign investment, trade agreements, and movement of goods/services/capital.</td>\n<td>Connections became faster, broader, and more digitally coordinated.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. Capitalism, socialism, and communism</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Ownership / emphasis</th>\n<th>Simple way to distinguish</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Capitalism</td>\n<td>Means of production are privately owned; profit, competition, and market choices are emphasized.</td>\n<td>Private ownership and competition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Socialism</td>\n<td>Means of production are collectively owned or socially controlled; collective goals and meeting basic needs are emphasized.</td>\n<td>Collective ownership and social welfare.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Communism</td>\n<td>In Marxist theory, an envisioned classless society in which members are socially equal; Marx treated socialism as a stage toward it.</td>\n<td>The theoretical classless endpoint in the source’s account.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lesson links capitalism to Adam Smith’s argument that competition and consumer choice can encourage efficiency. It describes socialism as rejecting a purely private-property, hands-off approach and placing more emphasis on shared goals. The source notes that real-world countries have not perfectly matched the theoretical model. Avoid treating any country’s actual system as a simple, pure example. (M4T15.pdf, pp. 5–8)</p>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> Capitalism and socialism are broad economic models, not historical stages of globalization. The timeline describes how global connections expanded; the economic models describe how production and ownership are organized.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Global integration developed over centuries and accelerated with industrial technology, postwar institutions, trade agreements, transportation, and the internet.</p>\n<h2>Topic 16: Global Corporations and the Role of Global Cities</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M4T16.pdf, pp. 1–14</em></p>\n<h3>A. Global corporations</h3>\n<p>Global corporations are large companies that operate in multiple countries. They may manufacture, distribute, market, or sell products across borders and locate facilities where labor, resources, infrastructure, or incentives are favorable.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Distinction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Multinational corporation (MNC)</td>\n<td>Company operating in more than one country, commonly with a home-country headquarters or identity.</td>\n<td>Has a recognizable home-country base.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Transnational corporation (TNC)</td>\n<td>A type of multinational corporation described in the lesson as more borderless and not centered on one national home.</td>\n<td>Emphasizes cross-border organization beyond a single national base.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global corporation</td>\n<td>Broad term for a company operating internationally.</td>\n<td>Can be used as an umbrella term in the lesson.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Examples named in the PDF include McDonald’s, Apple, Toyota, Coca-Cola, and Samsung. Positive effects can include more employment, lower prices, increased output, better resource allocation, and access to international products. The lesson also highlights cultural exchange through trade, mass media, and the internet. (M4T16.pdf, pp. 5–9)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Potential benefits: investment, jobs, access to products and technologies, larger markets, and knowledge transfer.</p></li>\n<li><p>Potential concerns to consider: dependence on foreign firms, pressure to keep labor costs low, uneven distribution of profits, and influence over local culture or policy.</p></li>\n<li><p>Companies may use incentives such as tax-free trade zones or lower operating costs when choosing locations.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>B. Global cities</h3>\n<p>A global city (or world city) is a major urban center with significant influence on international economic activity, politics, culture, and communication. It connects regions through finance, trade, transportation, technology, international organizations, and talent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Centers of international business and finance.</p></li>\n<li><p>Headquarters of multinational corporations and financial institutions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Strong transport and communication networks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Attract workers, students, investors, entrepreneurs, and skilled professionals from around the world.</p></li>\n<li><p>Centers of culture, education, innovation, and media.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examples in the source: New York City, London, Tokyo, Singapore, Paris, and Hong Kong. Their rise is associated with expanding trade, ports and airports, global corporations, finance and banking, telecommunications, migration, and the location of governments or international institutions. (M4T16.pdf, pp. 10–14)</p>\n<p><strong>UNDERSTAND THIS —</strong> A global corporation is a company; a global city is a place that concentrates and connects international activity. They support one another: corporations use global cities for finance, talent, transport, and communication, while cities gain influence from hosting global firms.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Global corporations and global cities are major connectors in globalization, but the economic and cultural gains may be distributed unevenly.</p>\n<h1>MODULE 5 — GLOBAL GOVERNANCE AND THE STATE</h1>\n<h2>Topic 17: Global Governance in the 21st Century</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M5T17.pdf, pp. 1–15; aditional notes.pdf, pp. 1–2</em></p>\n<h3>A. Meaning and purpose</h3>\n<p>Global governance refers to the management and decision-making processes involving governments, international organizations, non-governmental organizations, businesses, individuals, and other actors at the global level. It coordinates responses to problems that cross borders. It is not the same as a single world government: there is no one global authority with full power over every country.</p>\n<p><strong>MUST MEMORIZE —</strong> Global governance = institutions, rules, norms, and processes that enable cooperation on cross-border issues.</p>\n<h3>B. Why global governance emerged</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Nation-states face limits when problems cross borders.</p></li>\n<li><p>Goods, capital, people, and digital information flow across national boundaries.</p></li>\n<li><p>Migration and refugee movements require cooperation between countries.</p></li>\n<li><p>Some governments cause or cannot control severe internal crises.</p></li>\n<li><p>Global financial crises spread beyond individual countries.</p></li>\n<li><p>Pandemics, climate change, human-rights violations, and environmental problems need coordinated responses.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>C. Actors that affect governance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Actor</th>\n<th>How it influences global affairs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Nation-states / governments</td>\n<td>Make laws, negotiate treaties, enforce policy within their territories, and represent national interests.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>International organizations</td>\n<td>Create forums, rules, programs, and coordination mechanisms. Examples: UN, WTO, WHO.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Multinational corporations</td>\n<td>Influence jobs, investment, production, supply chains, and sometimes public policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>NGOs and civil society</td>\n<td>Provide humanitarian services, advocate for rights, monitor institutions, and mobilize public attention. Examples in the materials include Red Cross and Amnesty International.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Non-state violent organizations</td>\n<td>Can threaten security and seek to challenge or replace governments; the lesson names Al-Qaeda and ISIS as examples.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Individuals and social movements</td>\n<td>Raise awareness, advocate for change, and mobilize across borders, including through online networks.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>D. Challenges to global governance</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Challenge</th>\n<th>Why it is difficult</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>No world government</td>\n<td>International organizations often depend on states’ cooperation; enforcement is limited.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>National sovereignty</td>\n<td>Governments may resist rules they believe interfere with independence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Unequal power</td>\n<td>Wealthy or powerful countries may have greater influence in decision-making.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Global inequality</td>\n<td>Countries differ in wealth, technology, healthcare, and resources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Geopolitical rivalry</td>\n<td>Wars, territorial disputes, and competition can block cooperation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Climate and environmental issues</td>\n<td>Countries may disagree about responsibility and the cost of solutions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Human-rights violations</td>\n<td>External pressure can conflict with claims of sovereignty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Economic instability</td>\n<td>Crises, debt, trade disputes, and unequal growth require coordinated responses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Migration and refugees</td>\n<td>Countries disagree over borders, responsibility, and protection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Institutional weaknesses</td>\n<td>Bureaucracy, political disagreement, and enforcement limits reduce effectiveness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Pandemics and health threats</td>\n<td>Countries must share information, resources, vaccines, and expertise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Corruption and accountability</td>\n<td>People may question whether decisions are transparent and decision-makers answerable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> Global governance is not global government. Governance describes coordination through many institutions and actors; a world government would be a single central authority.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Global governance exists because many modern problems are transnational, but cooperation is difficult because states have different interests and unequal power.</p>\n<h2>Topic 18: Effects of Globalization on Governments</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M5T18-1.pdf, pp. 1–14</em></p>\n<h3>A. Traditional challenges: national security and territorial integrity</h3>\n<p>A sovereign state faces threats when another country intervenes militarily or attempts to control its territory. The lesson uses Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, Israel’s operations in Gaza, and a description of Israel–Lebanon events as examples of disputes involving security, territorial control, displacement, and sovereignty. Current-event descriptions in the PDF are time-sensitive; treat them as examples in the course material, not as a live update.</p>\n<p><strong>MUST MEMORIZE —</strong> Sovereignty is a state’s authority to govern itself; territorial integrity is the principle that a state’s territory should not be seized or violated by another state.</p>\n<h3>B. National and identity movements</h3>\n<p>A nation is a group with a shared identity, culture, or sense of belonging; a state is a political entity with defined territory and government. These do not always match. People who share an identity may live across multiple states, or a region may seek greater autonomy or independence.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Movement / example in source</th>\n<th>Main issue illustrated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Kurdish nationalist movement</td>\n<td>Kurds live across Turkey, Iraq, Iran, and Syria; advocacy includes cultural rights, recognition, autonomy, and for some groups independence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Catalan independence movement</td>\n<td>Catalan identity and political demands for autonomy or independence within Spain.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Scottish nationalism</td>\n<td>The 2014 referendum rejected independence; Scotland nevertheless has devolved powers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Indigenous rights movements</td>\n<td>Land rights, cultural preservation, representation, and recognition of traditional territories.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Black Lives Matter</td>\n<td>Racial equality and opposition to systemic racism; the movement gained international influence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Māori rights movement</td>\n<td>Indigenous rights, language revitalization, cultural preservation, and Treaty of Waitangi obligations in New Zealand.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Dalit rights movement</td>\n<td>Opposition to caste-based discrimination and pursuit of social, political, and economic equality in India.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Tibetan autonomy/independence movements</td>\n<td>Preservation of Tibetan culture, religion, language, and political rights; views differ over autonomy versus independence.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. Major effects of globalization on governments</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Impact</th>\n<th>Potential benefit</th>\n<th>Potential challenge</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Economic growth and trade</td>\n<td>More exports, investment, jobs, and tax revenue.</td>\n<td>Dependence on foreign investment and global market shocks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Reduced control over economy</td>\n<td>Can attract mobile capital and new business.</td>\n<td>Money and companies can move across borders; external rules may shape policy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International cooperation</td>\n<td>Joint responses to climate change, terrorism, pandemics, migration, and crime.</td>\n<td>Countries may disagree on responsibilities and priorities.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Policy changes</td>\n<td>Governments may improve infrastructure or revise regulations to attract investors.</td>\n<td>Policies may prioritize competitiveness over other local needs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Technology and information</td>\n<td>Better access to expertise and digital public services.</td>\n<td>Unequal access and privacy risks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Competition between countries</td>\n<td>Can encourage investment in education, infrastructure, and services.</td>\n<td>Pressure to lower costs or compete for skilled workers and firms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sovereignty challenges</td>\n<td>Shared rules can help solve common problems.</td>\n<td>International commitments can limit some policy choices.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Social and cultural change</td>\n<td>Cultural exchange and new ideas.</td>\n<td>Debates about identity, migration, and cultural change.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Globalization does not affect governments in only one direction. It can expand opportunities and cooperation while also constraining policy choices and exposing states to external pressures.</p>\n<h2>Topic 19: The Relevance of the State amid Globalization</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M5T19.pdf, pp. 1–10; aditional notes.pdf, p. 2</em></p>\n<h3>A. State, nation, and nation-state</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Example / cue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>State</td>\n<td>A political community with government, territory, rules, and sovereignty.</td>\n<td>A political unit that governs a territory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Nation</td>\n<td>People connected by shared culture, history, identity, or belonging.</td>\n<td>A nation can exist across state borders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Nation-state</td>\n<td>A state in which political boundaries and a shared national identity are understood to align.</td>\n<td>A common government and national identity.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The nation-state historically helped create a unified identity and sense of belonging. Globalization has made its role more complex because economic, social, and cultural connections increasingly cross borders. Some scholars argue that globalization weakens states; others argue that states remain central or that power becomes concentrated in particularly influential states, financial centers, or corporations.</p>\n<h3>B. Arguments about state power</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Main argument</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Hyperglobalist view</td>\n<td>Globalization has made the state less necessary or less powerful because markets and networks cross borders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>State-centered / skeptical view</td>\n<td>States remain powerful; global processes are sometimes exaggerated and domestic economic activity can remain larger than cross-border activity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Middle position</td>\n<td>Globalization changes the state’s role rather than simply eliminating it. States regulate, negotiate, cooperate, and respond to global pressures.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The supplementary notes present the nation-state’s role as largely regulatory in a globally interdependent world. States that were previously isolated must now engage with others to set international commerce policies. (aditional notes.pdf, p. 2)</p>\n<h3>C. Béland and collective insecurity</h3>\n<p>The lesson cites Béland (2008), who argues that the state’s role endures and may even increase in advanced industrial societies. Four sources of collective insecurity are listed: terrorism; economic globalization and pressures such as outsourcing, downsizing, and economic crisis; threats to national identity associated with immigration; and global diseases such as AIDS. The lesson also cites Glassner (2000) for the idea that states may sometimes exaggerate or create fears, not only respond to them. (M5T19.pdf, pp. 5–7)</p>\n<p><strong>UNDERSTAND THIS —</strong> Globalization can reduce a state’s control over some economic processes, but it can also increase the need for state regulation, public protection, crisis response, and international diplomacy.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> The state has not simply disappeared. Its role changes as it manages interdependence, protects citizens, regulates markets, and negotiates with global actors.</p>\n<h2>Topic 20: Institutions that Govern International Relations</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M5T20.pdf, pp. 1–20; aditional notes.pdf, p. 3</em></p>\n<h3>A. International relations (IR)</h3>\n<p>International relations refers to how countries interact, and how governments, international organizations, and other global actors cooperate or handle conflict.</p>\n<p>Why IR matters: it supports peace and security; promotes economic cooperation; addresses global problems; protects national interests; supports international law; and strengthens international organizations.</p>\n<h3>B. Major organizations and their roles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Organization</th>\n<th>Role / focus</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>United Nations (UN)</td>\n<td>Global forum for peace and security, human rights, development, humanitarian action, and international cooperation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>World Trade Organization (WTO)</td>\n<td>Trade rules, negotiations, and trade disputes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International Monetary Fund (IMF)</td>\n<td>International monetary and financial stability; support for countries facing financial difficulty.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>World Bank</td>\n<td>Development financing, infrastructure, poverty reduction, education, and related projects.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>World Health Organization (WHO)</td>\n<td>International public health and responses to disease outbreaks and health emergencies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)</td>\n<td>Political and military alliance based on collective defense among members.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN)</td>\n<td>Regional cooperation among Southeast Asian countries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>European Union (EU)</td>\n<td>Regional political and economic integration among European member states.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International Court of Justice (ICJ)</td>\n<td>Principal judicial organ of the UN; settles legal disputes between states and issues advisory opinions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>UNICEF</td>\n<td>UN agency focused on children’s well-being and support, including emergency relief and education.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>UN Environment Programme (UNEP)</td>\n<td>Coordinates environmental work within the UN system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>IPCC</td>\n<td>Assesses scientific knowledge about climate change; it is not itself a court or a general-purpose government.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>NGOs</td>\n<td>Independent, typically nonprofit organizations operating outside direct government control, often in humanitarian, social, or environmental work.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Red Cross / Red Crescent</td>\n<td>Humanitarian relief and support in disasters and conflict; the lesson emphasizes neutrality and aid to wounded people on all sides.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. The United Nations</h3>\n<p>The PDF says the term “United Nations” was coined by US President Franklin D. Roosevelt in 1942; the organization began operations on 24 October 1945 after World War II. The lesson describes the UN as a forum where states discuss issues without resorting to war. It lists 193 member states. (M5T20.pdf, pp. 8–10)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Security Council: responsible for international peace and security. The source describes 15 members: five permanent members plus ten elected members serving two-year terms.</p></li>\n<li><p>Five permanent members named in the source: United States, United Kingdom, Russia, China, and France. Each permanent member has veto power on substantive Council decisions.</p></li>\n<li><p>General Assembly: forum for member states to express views and seek agreement.</p></li>\n<li><p>UNICEF: supports children, including emergency relief and education.</p></li>\n<li><p>Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs): broad goals covering economic prosperity, social well-being, and environmental protection.</p></li>\n<li><p>UNEP and the wider UN climate system address environmental issues; the source names the IPCC in connection with climate change.</p></li>\n<li><p>ICJ: based in The Hague, Netherlands, and settles legal disputes between states. The source also mentions ITLOS, which addresses disputes under the law of the sea.</p></li>\n<li><p>Human-rights mechanisms named include OHCHR, the Human Rights Council, human-rights treaty bodies, and other UN mechanisms.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> The ICJ is the UN’s principal court for disputes between states. It is not the same institution as the International Criminal Court (ICC), which prosecutes individuals for certain international crimes. The source’s wording about “war criminals and rulers” may blur this distinction.</p>\n<h3>D. NATO and NGOs</h3>\n<p>NATO is a defensive military and political alliance built around collective security: members agree to cooperate in defending one another under the treaty’s terms. An NGO is not a treaty alliance; it is an independent organization, often nonprofit, that may provide services, advocate, or monitor conditions. The Red Cross is used in the lesson as an example of neutral humanitarian assistance. (M5T20.pdf, pp. 18–20)</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> International relations are shaped by states and a range of institutions. Different organizations have different mandates—trade, finance, health, security, development, law, or humanitarian assistance—so they should not be treated as interchangeable.</p>\n<h1>MODULE 6 — GLOBAL CITIZENSHIP, INFORMATION, AND AI</h1>\n<h2>Topic 21: Global Citizenship</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M6T21.pdf, pp. 1–14; aditional notes.pdf, p. 3</em></p>\n<h3>A. Definition and main idea</h3>\n<p>Global citizenship is the idea that people see themselves as members of a worldwide community, not only as citizens of one country. It recognizes that people and countries are interconnected and that choices can affect others locally, nationally, and internationally.</p>\n<p>Caecilia Johanna van Peski (2012) defines global citizenship as a moral and ethical disposition that helps people understand local and global contexts and their responsibilities within different communities. The lesson also notes that globalization is not one single process; there are many “globalizations.”</p>\n<p><strong>MUST MEMORIZE —</strong> Global citizenship combines awareness of global interconnectedness with a sense of responsibility toward other people and the planet.</p>\n<h3>B. Four types of global citizenship</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Illustrative action</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ethical</td>\n<td>Empathy, justice, human rights, and responsible conduct.</td>\n<td>Treating people fairly and supporting human dignity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Cultural</td>\n<td>Understanding and appreciating diverse cultures; dialogue, tolerance, and respect.</td>\n<td>Listening respectfully to classmates from different backgrounds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Civic</td>\n<td>Participation in global issues, social justice, and political processes.</td>\n<td>Joining a lawful awareness campaign or contacting representatives.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Economic</td>\n<td>Global economy, fair trade, sustainable development, and economic equality.</td>\n<td>Considering labor conditions and sustainability when buying products.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. Six domains</h3>\n<ol start=\"7\" type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Environmental stewardship</p></li>\n<li><p>Human rights</p></li>\n<li><p>Gender equity</p></li>\n<li><p>Good governance</p></li>\n<li><p>Global peace and justice</p></li>\n<li><p>Poverty reduction</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>D. Global citizenship education (GCED)</h3>\n<p>Global citizenship education helps learners understand the interconnected world and develop the knowledge, values, and skills needed to participate responsibly. The source says GCED overlaps with or can encompass multicultural education, peace education, human-rights education, education for sustainable development, and international education.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Curriculum development: teach global interdependence and contemporary challenges.</p></li>\n<li><p>Human rights and justice: examine rights, geography, inequality, and history.</p></li>\n<li><p>Intercultural understanding: develop respect, empathy, and open-mindedness.</p></li>\n<li><p>Peace and conflict resolution: resolve disagreements constructively.</p></li>\n<li><p>Sustainable development: understand environmental issues and sustainable practices.</p></li>\n<li><p>Global issues: study poverty, inequality, climate change, and conflict.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The source cites Audrey Osler, who argues that education for living together in an interdependent world is essential rather than optional. It also describes teachers as potential agents of social change. (M6T21.pdf, pp. 11–14)</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Global citizenship is not about replacing national citizenship. It adds a global perspective to people’s responsibilities, encouraging informed, respectful, and constructive action.</p>\n<h2>Topic 22: Informationalism</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M6T22-1.pdf, pp. 1–20; aditional notes.pdf, p. 4</em></p>\n<h3>A. Definition and origins</h3>\n<p>Informationalism is a societal shift in which knowledge, information, and communication technologies become central to economic, social, and cultural development. The lesson associates the concept with Manuel Castells and describes it as a technological paradigm associated with computer science, telecommunications, media, and the internet that replaces the earlier emphasis of industrialism.</p>\n<p><strong>MUST MEMORIZE —</strong> Informationalism = a social and economic order in which information and digital communication technologies are central.</p>\n<h3>B. Key characteristics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Knowledge and information are created, stored, retrieved, and shared at high speed.</p></li>\n<li><p>Technology transforms business, education, government, and everyday life.</p></li>\n<li><p>Networked relationships mediate social, political, and economic activity.</p></li>\n<li><p>Digital networks connect people across geography and time zones.</p></li>\n<li><p>The internet enables broad participation in theory, but access and skills remain unequal.</p></li>\n<li><p>Information shared online can expose users to privacy risks, spam, malware, and misinformation.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lesson cites McLuhan and Fiore (2005) for the idea that the medium—the way a message is transmitted—shapes people and culture, not just the content. It also cites Ritzer (2015) on the internet and the “flat world” thesis, while acknowledging that access is limited by electricity, literacy, financial systems, and government regulations. (M6T22-1.pdf, pp. 8–11)</p>\n<h3>C. Network society vs information capitalism</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network society</td>\n<td>How society is organized through digital networks linking people, organizations, and activities.</td>\n<td>A company coordinates staff in several countries through online platforms and cloud services.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Information capitalism</td>\n<td>An economic system in which information, data, knowledge, and digital technologies are major sources of value and power.</td>\n<td>A platform analyzes user data and earns advertising revenue.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In a network society, people and organizations are digitally connected; information capitalism describes how businesses can turn information and digital services into economic value. The concepts overlap but answer different questions: “How is society organized?” versus “How is value created and captured?”</p>\n<h3>D. Concerns and criticisms</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Digital inequality: some people lack reliable internet, devices, electricity, or digital skills.</p></li>\n<li><p>Privacy: organizations may collect and analyze personal information.</p></li>\n<li><p>Surveillance: companies or governments may monitor online behavior.</p></li>\n<li><p>Job displacement: automation can replace or change some tasks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Economic concentration: firms controlling data and infrastructure can gain power.</p></li>\n<li><p>Misinformation: connected networks can spread false content quickly.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> Informationalism is the broad shift toward information-centered society; a network society is the way social organization is structured through networks; information capitalism is the way economic value is generated through data and digital systems.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Digital networks connect people and institutions, but connectivity alone does not guarantee equal access, privacy, or fair distribution of economic power.</p>\n<h2>Topic 23: The Rise of Artificial Intelligence</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M6T23.pdf, pp. 1–21; aditional notes.pdf, p. 4</em></p>\n<h3>A. What AI is and why it matters</h3>\n<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) refers to computer systems designed to perform tasks associated with human intelligence, such as learning, recognizing patterns, understanding language, making predictions, generating content, and assisting decisions. The lesson describes AI’s rise as a rapid development that now affects daily life, work, communication, education, healthcare, finance, government, and entertainment.</p>\n<h3>B. A short history of AI</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Period / stage</th>\n<th>Development</th>\n<th>Main lesson</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Philosophical roots and early computing</td>\n<td>People asked whether machines could think; 1940s computing helped turn the question into a scientific project.</td>\n<td>AI began with questions about reasoning and intelligence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>1956: birth of AI as a field</td>\n<td>Dartmouth workshop formally established AI research as a field. Early systems focused on symbols, logic, rules, and problem-solving.</td>\n<td>Researchers tried to program intelligence through explicit rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>First AI winter (1970s)</td>\n<td>Early optimism met limits: systems struggled with ambiguity, uncertainty, context, and common sense; funding and enthusiasm declined.</td>\n<td>Human intelligence was harder to capture with simple rules than expected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>1980s–1990s: rules to learning</td>\n<td>Machine learning increasingly allowed computers to identify patterns in data instead of relying only on hand-coded rules.</td>\n<td>Data became central to system performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>2010s: deep learning</td>\n<td>Large datasets, powerful hardware, and neural networks improved image recognition, speech, translation, and complex tasks. In 2016, AlphaGo defeated professional Go champion Lee Sedol.</td>\n<td>Systems could learn complex patterns and strategies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2020s: generative AI</td>\n<td>Systems began generating text, images, music, video, code, and other content.</td>\n<td>AI became a more general-purpose creative and intellectual tool.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>MEMORY AID —</strong> Rules → Learning → Neural networks / deep learning → Generative models.</p>\n<h3>C. Uses of AI in society</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Uses described in the lesson</th>\n<th>Important caution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Personalized learning, tutoring, feedback, translation, content assistance.</td>\n<td>AI should support learning, not replace independent thought.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Healthcare</td>\n<td>Medical imaging, disease detection, drug discovery, patient monitoring, administration.</td>\n<td>Errors, bias, privacy, and accountability matter greatly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Business and employment</td>\n<td>Customer service, marketing, fraud detection, analysis, recruitment, forecasting, reports, programming.</td>\n<td>Productivity gains may accompany job transformation or displacement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Communication and social media</td>\n<td>Recommendation algorithms, translation, spam detection, moderation, content generation.</td>\n<td>Algorithms influence what information people see.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Finance</td>\n<td>Fraud detection, risk assessment, credit analysis, trading, customer support.</td>\n<td>Biased or opaque decisions can harm users.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Government and public services</td>\n<td>Public administration, disaster prediction, traffic management, service delivery, planning.</td>\n<td>Strong safeguards are needed because decisions affect rights and access.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Entertainment and creative industries</td>\n<td>Images, scripts, music, voices, games, and other creative material.</td>\n<td>Raises questions about authorship and creativity.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The PDF’s numbering skips a visible category between social media and finance; this guide preserves the categories actually present in the extracted pages rather than inventing a missing heading. (M6T23.pdf, pp. 14–21)</p>\n<p><strong>UNDERSTAND THIS —</strong> AI is not a single technology. It includes different methods and systems. A tool that classifies an image, a system that recommends a video, and a generative model that writes a paragraph perform different tasks.</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> AI developed from rule-based approaches toward data-driven learning and generative systems. Its social effects depend on how people design, deploy, supervise, and govern it.</p>\n<h2>Topic 24: Ethical and Social Issues Related to AI</h2>\n<p><em>Source: M6T24.pdf, pp. 1–14</em></p>\n<h3>A. Ten key concerns</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Issue</th>\n<th>Core concern</th>\n<th>Example / question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>1. Privacy and data protection</td>\n<td>AI often needs data; people may not know how it is collected or used.</td>\n<td>Does a facial-recognition system collect or use personal information without meaningful control?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>2. Bias and discrimination</td>\n<td>Biased human data can produce unfair AI outcomes.</td>\n<td>Could a hiring algorithm unfairly disadvantage a group?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>3. Employment and job displacement</td>\n<td>Automation can perform tasks formerly done by people.</td>\n<td>How can workers retrain as jobs change?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>4. Accountability and responsibility</td>\n<td>It may be unclear who is responsible when AI causes harm.</td>\n<td>Developer, hospital, professional, or organization—who must answer for a harmful recommendation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>5. Transparency and explainability</td>\n<td>Some systems are “black boxes” whose decisions are difficult to explain.</td>\n<td>Can a person understand why a loan application was rejected?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>6. Misinformation and deepfakes</td>\n<td>AI can create convincing false images, video, audio, and text.</td>\n<td>How can people verify a voice recording or video?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>7. Human autonomy and dependence</td>\n<td>Overreliance can weaken independent judgment and critical thinking.</td>\n<td>Does a person accept an AI answer without checking it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>8. Inequality and the digital divide</td>\n<td>Benefits may go to those with better technology, skills, and infrastructure.</td>\n<td>Will poorer communities be excluded from AI’s benefits?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>9. Security and misuse</td>\n<td>AI may enable scams, cyberattacks, and automated manipulation.</td>\n<td>How can harmful uses be limited?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>10. Human dignity and the meaning of humanity</td>\n<td>AI challenges ideas about creativity, intelligence, and human value.</td>\n<td>Should human qualities be treated as merely technical functions?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>B. Ethical questions to remember</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Related principle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Who controls AI?</td>\n<td>Power and governance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Who benefits from AI?</td>\n<td>Equality and economic justice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Who is responsible when AI causes harm?</td>\n<td>Accountability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>How does AI use our information?</td>\n<td>Privacy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Can AI treat everyone fairly?</td>\n<td>Bias and discrimination.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Can we trust AI decisions?</td>\n<td>Transparency and explainability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Will AI replace or transform human work?</td>\n<td>Employment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Can humans remain autonomous?</td>\n<td>Human agency.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Can we trust what we see and hear?</td>\n<td>Misinformation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>What should AI never be allowed to do?</td>\n<td>Ethics and human values.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>C. Principles for responsible AI</h3>\n<p>The lesson concludes that AI is a human and societal issue, not merely a technical one. The goal is not necessarily to stop progress, but to develop and use AI according to fairness, privacy, transparency, accountability, safety, human dignity, and social responsibility. Success should be measured not only by machine capability but also by the wisdom with which people use it. (M6T24.pdf, pp. 13–14)</p>\n<p><strong>KEY TAKEAWAY —</strong> Responsible AI requires human oversight, clear accountability, fairness, protection of data, explainable high-impact decisions, and safeguards against misuse.</p>\n<h1>SUPPLEMENTARY NOTES — UNIQUE AND OVERLAPPING TOPICS</h1>\n<p><em>Source: aditional notes.pdf, pp. 1–4; Cyberactivism.pdf, pp. 1–2</em></p>\n<h2>1. Global governance in the 20th century</h2>\n<p>The notes define global governance as activity at international, transnational, and regional levels involving both public and private sectors across national boundaries. It is not a single integrated world system. The notes describe modern governance as often bureaucratic and rule-based: regularized, codified, and organized through procedures. (aditional notes.pdf, p. 1; James &amp; Soguk, 2014, as cited in the notes)</p>\n<h2>2. Non-governmental organizations (NGOs)</h2>\n<p>NGOs are organizations independent of direct government involvement, usually nonprofit, founded by citizens or groups to provide services, advocate, or address humanitarian, social, or environmental issues. They may receive funding from different sources. Their public trust can make them useful representatives of social concerns, but they are still organizations whose actions and funding can be examined critically. (aditional notes.pdf, p. 1)</p>\n<h2>3. Global social movements (GSMs)</h2>\n<p>Global social movements are networks of individuals and organizations collaborating across borders and beyond national identities to pursue similar goals around the world. They may use advocacy, public campaigns, demonstrations, research, and online communication. They connect to global citizenship, identity movements, NGOs, and cyberactivism. (aditional notes.pdf, p. 2)</p>\n<h2>4. Spread of global diseases</h2>\n<p>Globalization increases the movement of people, goods, capital, and information across borders. This can help spread infectious diseases rapidly, but international connections can also support public-health cooperation and help alleviate factors such as hunger and poverty that influence health. (aditional notes.pdf, p. 2)</p>\n<h2>5. Cyberactivism</h2>\n<p>Cyberactivism is the use of internet-based communication and social technologies to create, organize, manage, or promote activism. It is also called internet activism, online activism, digital activism, online organizing, electronic advocacy, e-campaigning, or e-activism. It lets individuals and organizations reach followers, broadcast messages, and promote causes or movements. (Cyberactivism.pdf, p. 1; aditional notes.pdf, p. 4)</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Potential strengths</th>\n<th>Potential limitations / risks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Fast spread of information and awareness.</td>\n<td>False or misleading information can spread quickly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Can connect supporters across distance.</td>\n<td>Online reach does not always produce offline change.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Allows individuals or small groups to participate.</td>\n<td>Harassment, privacy risks, platform rules, and unequal internet access can limit participation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Can help coordinate campaigns and share evidence.</td>\n<td>Messages can be manipulated, oversimplified, or lost in attention competition.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The Cyberactivism PDF names Global Huaren as an example of an organization promoting the well-being of Huaren (Chinese) and non-Huaren around the world and cultivating a sense of global citizenship. The notes also say that future globalization may have multiple possible paths: some expect further expansion, while others imagine a more pessimistic disruption of the current global order. (Cyberactivism.pdf, p. 2)</p>\n<p><strong>COMMONLY CONFUSED —</strong> Cyberactivism is a method or channel for activism, while a global social movement is a cross-border network pursuing a shared agenda. Cyberactivism can help a movement organize, but the two terms are not identical.</p>\n<h2>6. Overlap map</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Connects with</th>\n<th>Shared idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global governance</td>\n<td>International relations; NGOs; state sovereignty; global social movements</td>\n<td>Cross-border problems require coordination among many actors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>State amid globalization</td>\n<td>Government impacts; national identity movements; international institutions</td>\n<td>States remain important but operate under global pressures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global citizenship</td>\n<td>GCED; human rights; sustainability; cyberactivism</td>\n<td>Individuals have responsibilities beyond their immediate community.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Informationalism</td>\n<td>Network society; information capitalism; AI; cyberactivism</td>\n<td>Digital information networks shape society, power, and participation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>AI ethics</td>\n<td>Privacy; digital divide; misinformation; employment; accountability</td>\n<td>Technology’s benefits and harms depend on governance and human choices.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h1>FINAL CRAM SHEET</h1>\n<h2>1. Essential definitions</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>One-line definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global market integration</td>\n<td>Connection of national markets so goods, services, money, technology, and information move more easily across borders.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>IFI</td>\n<td>International institution that supports financial stability or development.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Bretton Woods System</td>\n<td>Post-World War II monetary framework agreed in 1944; led to the IMF and IBRD/World Bank.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Capitalism</td>\n<td>Economic model emphasizing private ownership, profit, and competition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Socialism</td>\n<td>Economic model emphasizing collective/social ownership and shared goals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>MNC</td>\n<td>Company operating in multiple countries, often with a home-country base.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>TNC</td>\n<td>Cross-border company described as less centered on a single national home.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Global city</td>\n<td>Urban center with major international influence in finance, politics, culture, and connectivity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global governance</td>\n<td>Cross-border coordination through institutions, rules, norms, and processes; not a single world government.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Sovereignty</td>\n<td>A state’s authority to govern itself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Territorial integrity</td>\n<td>Protection of a state’s territory from seizure or violation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>State</td>\n<td>Political community with government and territory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Nation</td>\n<td>People connected by shared identity, culture, history, or belonging.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Nation-state</td>\n<td>State whose political community is associated with a shared national identity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>International relations</td>\n<td>Interactions among states and other global actors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Global citizenship</td>\n<td>Seeing oneself as part of a worldwide community with responsibilities to others and the planet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GCED</td>\n<td>Education that develops understanding, skills, and responsibility for an interconnected world.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Informationalism</td>\n<td>Societal shift placing information and communication technologies at the center of development.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Network society</td>\n<td>Society whose activities are increasingly organized through digital networks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Information capitalism</td>\n<td>Economic system in which data, knowledge, and digital services are major sources of value.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>AI</td>\n<td>Computer systems designed to perform tasks associated with learning, prediction, language, recognition, generation, or decision support.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Deepfake</td>\n<td>AI-generated or manipulated media that convincingly imitates real people or events.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Cyberactivism</td>\n<td>Use of online communication technologies to organize or promote activism.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>2. Lists worth memorizing</h2>\n<h3>Historical stages of global market integration</h3>\n<ol start=\"13\" type=\"1\">\n<li><p>Early trade routes</p></li>\n<li><p>Age of Exploration</p></li>\n<li><p>Industrial Revolution</p></li>\n<li><p>Late 19th–early 20th-century expansion and disruption</p></li>\n<li><p>Bretton Woods / postwar system</p></li>\n<li><p>Trade growth (1950s–1980s)</p></li>\n<li><p>Modern globalization (1990s–present in the source)</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Three sectors of the economy</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Primary = extracts raw materials.</p></li>\n<li><p>Secondary = manufactures goods from materials.</p></li>\n<li><p>Tertiary = provides services.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>IMF vs World Bank vs ADB</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><p>IMF = monetary/financial stability and crisis assistance.</p></li>\n<li><p>World Bank = long-term development projects and poverty reduction.</p></li>\n<li><p>ADB = development in Asia and the Pacific.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Global governance challenges</h3>\n<p>No world government; sovereignty; unequal power; global inequality; geopolitical rivalry; climate/environment; human rights; economic instability; migration/refugees; institutional weaknesses; pandemics; corruption/accountability.</p>\n<h3>Six domains of global citizenship</h3>\n<p>Environmental stewardship; human rights; gender equity; good governance; global peace and justice; poverty reduction.</p>\n<h3>AI history sequence</h3>\n<p>Philosophical roots / early computing → 1956 Dartmouth birth of AI → AI winter → machine learning → deep learning (2010s) → generative AI (2020s).</p>\n<h3>Ten AI ethical issues</h3>\n<p>Privacy; bias; employment; accountability; transparency; misinformation/deepfakes; autonomy/dependence; inequality/digital divide; security/misuse; human dignity.</p>\n<h2>3. Comparisons in one glance</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th>Do not confuse</th>\n<th>Difference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Global governance vs world government</td>\n<td>Governance is coordination among multiple actors; world government would be one central authority.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Nation vs state</td>\n<td>Nation = shared identity; state = political-territorial organization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>MNC vs TNC</td>\n<td>MNC usually has a home-country base; TNC is described as more border-spanning and less tied to one national base.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Global corporation vs global city</td>\n<td>Corporation = company; city = urban hub connecting international activity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>IMF vs World Bank</td>\n<td>Financial stability/crisis support vs longer-term development and poverty reduction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>UN Security Council vs General Assembly</td>\n<td>Security Council handles peace/security decisions; General Assembly is a broad forum for member-state debate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>ICJ vs ICC</td>\n<td>ICJ settles legal disputes between states; ICC prosecutes individuals for certain international crimes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Global citizenship vs national citizenship</td>\n<td>Global citizenship adds a worldwide perspective and responsibility; it does not automatically replace national citizenship.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Informationalism vs network society vs information capitalism</td>\n<td>Information-centered social shift vs network-based social organization vs data-centered value creation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>AI vs generative AI</td>\n<td>AI is the broad field; generative AI is a category that creates new content.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Cyberactivism vs global social movement</td>\n<td>Online organizing method vs cross-border network of people and organizations pursuing a shared cause.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>4. Major cause-and-effect relationships</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Industrial Revolution → faster production and transport → larger-scale international trade.</p></li>\n<li><p>Bretton Woods → IMF and IBRD/World Bank → postwar financial cooperation and development support.</p></li>\n<li><p>Global market integration → more competition and consumer choice, but also greater interdependence and exposure to external shocks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Global corporations + transport + finance + communications → growth of global cities.</p></li>\n<li><p>Cross-border problems → need for global governance, but sovereignty and unequal power complicate cooperation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Globalization → economic opportunities and cooperation, but also policy constraints and competition among states.</p></li>\n<li><p>Digital networks → rapid information exchange and new business models, but also privacy risks, digital inequality, surveillance, and misinformation.</p></li>\n<li><p>More capable AI → productivity and new services, but also bias, job changes, accountability questions, and potential misuse.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. One-sentence lesson summaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Topic 14: IFIs and economic organizations help countries finance development, stabilize finance, and coordinate trade, although the fairness of the system is debated.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 15: Global market integration grew through trade routes, exploration, industrialization, postwar institutions, and digital globalization.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 16: Global corporations operate across borders while global cities concentrate the finance, talent, infrastructure, and culture that connect the world.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 17: Global governance coordinates transnational issues through many actors, but has no single world government and faces enforcement and power problems.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 18: Globalization gives governments new opportunities and cooperation channels while challenging their control, security, and sovereignty.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 19: States remain important, but their roles change as they regulate, negotiate, and respond to global interdependence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 20: International organizations have distinct mandates in security, trade, finance, health, law, development, and humanitarian relief.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 21: Global citizenship means recognizing global connections and acting responsibly toward people and the planet.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 22: Informationalism makes information and digital networks central to society and the economy, while raising concerns about inequality and privacy.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 23: AI evolved from rule-based systems to machine learning, deep learning, and generative models used across everyday life.</p></li>\n<li><p>Topic 24: AI should be governed with fairness, privacy, transparency, accountability, safety, human dignity, and social responsibility.</p></li>\n<li><p>Supplementary: Cyberactivism uses online networks to organize and promote causes; it can amplify movements but does not guarantee accurate information or real-world change.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Quick source checklist</h2>\n<p>All uploaded PDFs are represented in the reviewer: M4T14-2, M4T15, M4T16, M5T17, M5T18-1, M5T19, M5T20, M6T21, M6T22-1, M6T23, M6T24, aditional notes, and Cyberactivism. Use the source page references under each topic to revisit original diagrams, tables, or phrasing in the PDF slides.</p>\n<p>End of reviewer.</p>\n", "notice": ""}};
